{"parentUuid":null,"isSidechain":true,"promptId":"bb7640f3-4c73-49eb-8121-41eedd8509ec","agentId":"a8e36db408996cf67","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":"Build Flint's reciprocal-links table for *Supertraining* Chapter 5, just promoted to the vault (SecondBrain commit 3109157). Write ONLY the table file; do not edit any vault or staging entry and do not run git.\n\nContext: 75 new entries (listed in `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/_scope_supertraining_ch5a.txt`, `_ch5b.txt`, `_ch5c.txt`) are now in the vault at `/Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/`. Older vault entries should now link back to them.\n\nRead first:\n- Format and conventions to copy exactly: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/reciprocal_links_2026-10-03.md` (the previous batch's table: header paragraph, per-book `##` sections, columns `| Source file | Target | Location | Related text to append | Origin |`, \"Dropped suggestions\", \"Manual edits (not handled by the script)\").\n- The consuming script: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/scripts/apply_reciprocal_links.py`. A Mac-path copy is at `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/_tmp/links_mac.py`; run it WITHOUT `--apply` as a dry run on your table: `cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint && ~/.local/bin/uv run python3 staging/_tmp/links_mac.py staging/reciprocal_links_2026-10-03-ch5.md`. It must report 0 unresolved.\n- Suggestions to evaluate: in the writers' and reviewers' material: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/review_supertraining_ch5a_opus.md`, `..._ch5a_opus_round2.md`, `..._ch5b_opus.md`, `..._ch5b_opus_round2.md`, `..._ch5c_opus.md`, `..._ch5c_opus_round2.md`, and the fix logs `fixes_supertraining_ch5a_applied.md`, `..._ch5b_...`, `..._ch5c_...` (all in `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/`). Also the writers' suggestion lists, reproduced here in short:\n  - 5a: force-time-profile principle -> Newton II entry; strength-detraining claim -> three-methods framework; threshold-stimulus claim -> tempo claim; two-force-regimes framework -> speed-strength loading heuristic; PAP framework and PAP-contested claim -> arm-throw case; isokinetic-testing heuristic -> APRE framework and APRE testing heuristic; five-criteria framework and Evans wrestling case -> not-copying heuristic; transfer-decreases claim -> independent-qualities principle and analytical-synthetic heuristic; Bompa LTAD framework and Issurin modality-compatibility principle -> analytical-synthetic heuristic; eight-types-of-tension framework and Zatsiorsky strength-speed correlation claim -> two-groups framework; velocity-specificity claim and eccentric-vs-concentric claim -> Ivanov claim (renamed `claim--ivanov-1966-contraction-mode-and-which-strength-test-improves`); Bondarchuk implement-weight heuristic and throws transfer claim -> implement-mass claim (renamed `claim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-keep-technique-and-size-per-athlete`); Bondarchuk sprint-jump claim and Moyer wearable-resistance claim -> bounding case; Moyer isometric set positions -> isometric APRE; Texas A&M VBT guide -> APRE framework.\n  - 5b: shock-method terminology framework -> coupling-time heuristic and classification framework; optimal drop-height claim and reactivity-coefficient framework -> height heuristic and intensity heuristic; strength-deficit heuristic -> brief-maximal-tension heuristic; plyometric joint-angle/fiber-type heuristic -> prestretch claim; strength-endurance taxonomy -> strength-endurance ceiling heuristic; haemoglobin/myoglobin claim -> hypertrophy claim; equal-force principle -> heavy-squats claim; Zatsiorsky reversible-action claim -> coupling-time and cognitive entries; Issurin residual-effects claim -> frequency heuristic.\n  - 5c: (see the 5c writer list in `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/review_supertraining_ch5c_opus.md` duplicates/missing sections and the 5c fix log). Include Issurin concentration-of-workloads -> conjugate sequence and concentrated-loading conditions; Issurin exercise-sequencing and concurrent-training case -> novice sequencing case and excess-heavy-strength claim; Issurin long-term four-stage and Bompa LTAD -> early specialisation; Bompa concentrated-loading-overreaching -> allow-restoration (CHECK: it was edited today and already links allow-restoration; if so, drop it as already linked); Bondarchuk sprint-strength transfer ceiling -> converging-partial-effects; Myszka donor-sport framework -> cross-training heuristic; research-papers Issurin 2016 brief -> conjugate sequence; plus Supertraining Ch1-4 sources the 5c writer named (continuous-vs-interval circuit claim, acute-delayed taxonomy, structured-load-progression heuristic, ES sequencing heuristic, four-level framework, functional-vs-nonfunctional machines, irradiation claim, parachute sizing, PAP framework).\n  - Ch4.1.1 `claim--delayed-strength-training-effect-lag-before-gains-appear` -> allow-restoration (round-2 5c note).\n  - The 5a depth-jump claim / 5b 475-jumps entry `claim--depth-jump-training-beat-higher-volume-traditional-training-in-jumpers` already links the 5c novice case? Check; if a new Ch5 entry should link another new Ch5 entry, those are in-batch and already reviewed; skip them unless the reviewers flagged them.\n\nFor every row: resolve both files by actual filename (use ls/grep; the short names above are descriptions, not slugs); confirm both exist in the vault; confirm the source resolves to exactly one file by `<book>/<file>.md` suffix; grep the source for an existing link to the target (if already linked, move to Dropped); read both files and write a one-line Related text that states the actual relationship accurately (no em dashes, plain style, `[[book/slug]]` cross-book, `[[slug]]` within the same book, match the source's link form). Sources must be OLD entries (not the 75 new ones). For text replacements (e.g. \"(see 5.x, not yet ingested)\" style placeholders in older Supertraining entries: grep the Supertraining folder for \"not yet ingested\" and \"Ch5\" / \"5.2.4\" / \"Chapter 5\" placeholders), put them under Manual edits with the exact current text and replacement, each confirmed to occur once. Drop suggestions the files do not support, with the reason. If a source has no **Related** paragraph or ends with stray content after it, note it.\n\nWrite `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/reciprocal_links_2026-10-03-ch5.md`. Final reply: script rows, manual edits, dropped (counts), the dry-run output line, and any doubts.\n<context_window_protection>\n  <priority_instructions>\n    Every byte a tool returns enters your conversation memory and costs reasoning capacity for the rest of the session. The context-mode tools let you do the work in a sandbox and surface only the derived answer — the raw bytes stay out. Think-in-Code: program the analysis, do not compute it by reading raw data into your conversation.\n  </priority_instructions>\n\n  <deferred_tool_bootstrap>\n    The context-mode tools below may be DEFERRED in your harness — their schemas are not loaded yet, so calling them directly fails (e.g. \"tool not found\" / InputValidationError). Load them ONCE before your first ctx_* call:\n    ToolSearch(query: \"select:mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute,mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search,mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute,mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file,mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index\")\n    After that they are callable. If any ctx_* call fails as not-found, ToolSearch it and retry — do NOT fall back to Bash/Read just because the schema was not loaded yet.\n  </deferred_tool_bootstrap>\n\n  <tool_selection_hierarchy>\n    0. MEMORY: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(sort: \"timeline\")\n       - On resume or compaction, query prior decisions, errors, plans, user prompts before asking the user — auto-captured session memory is searchable.\n    1. GATHER: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute(commands, queries)\n       - Primary research tool. Runs commands in parallel, auto-indexes each output, and (when queries are passed) returns matching sections in the same round trip — no follow-up search call.\n       - Each command: {label: \"section header\", command: \"shell command\"}; the label becomes the FTS5 chunk title — descriptive labels improve search.\n    2. FOLLOW-UP: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(queries: [\"q1\", \"q2\", ...])\n       - Multiple related questions about anything already indexed (your captures + session memory). Batch every question in one array; the ranking pipeline runs per-query and the round-trip cost is paid once.\n    3. PROCESSING: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute(language, code) | mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file(path, language, code)\n       - Derive answers FROM data: filter, count, aggregate, parse, transform. Only what you console.log() enters your conversation; the raw bytes stay in the sandbox.\n  </tool_selection_hierarchy>\n\n  <when_not_to_use>\n    - You intend to PROCESS the output (filter, count, parse, aggregate) → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute or mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute. Bash stays correct when you intend to OBSERVE a short fixed output (git status on a clean tree, whoami, pwd) or when you are mutating state (git, mkdir, rm, mv, navigation).\n    - You want to analyze, summarize, or extract from a file → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file. Read stays correct when you intend to Edit the file (Edit needs the exact bytes in your conversation to match against).\n    - WebFetch → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index; full network access, results indexed for mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search, raw page bytes never enter your conversation.\n    - mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute and mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file for file writes → these run code in a subprocess and discard the sandbox FS; they are for analysis, processing, and computation only.\n  </when_not_to_use>\n\n  <file_writing_policy>\n    File writes use the native Write or Edit tool — mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute, mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file, and Bash subprocesses do not persist edits to the host filesystem.\n    Applies to all file types: code, configs, plans, specs, YAML, JSON, markdown.\n  </file_writing_policy>\n\n  <output_constraints>\n    <artifact_policy>\n      Write artifacts (code, configs, PRDs) to files. Return only: file path + 1-line description.\n    </artifact_policy>\n  </output_constraints>\n  <session_continuity>\n    Skills, roles, and decisions captured earlier in this session are a memory aid, not a standing order. Treat them as context that may help — the user's most recent message always takes precedence. If a captured directive conflicts with what the user now asks, follow the user; a past phrase does not bind you.\n  </session_continuity>\n\n</context_window_protection>"},"uuid":"2003a467-3642-4e40-a532-a2d8a1b7ce82","timestamp":"2026-10-03T17:01:33.758Z","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
