{"parentUuid":"8ada193f-2a9a-4182-8ba5-838b13315027","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a8e36db408996cf67","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfffQqkzAqjHGjNY4zZKv","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_018B9kewQ2NCJK2p591CzmBB","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging && sed -n 40,62p review_supertraining_ch5a_opus.md; echo ----; sed -n 35,76p review_supertraining_ch5c_opus.md; echo ----; sed -n 55,86p review_supertraining_ch5b_opus.md; echo ---; sed -n 60,87p review_supertraining_ch5b_opus_round2.md","description":"Read reciprocal-link and duplicates sections of reviews"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2339,"cache_read_input_tokens":66989,"output_tokens":230,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2339},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":230,"cache_read_input_tokens":66989,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2339,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2339,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_018B9kewQ2NCJK2p591CzmBB":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging && sed -n 40,62p review_supertraining_ch5a_opus.md; echo ----; sed -n 35,76p review_supertraining_ch5c_opus.md; echo ----; sed -n 55,86p review_supertraining_ch5b_opus.md; echo ---; sed -n 60,87p review_supertraining_ch5b_opus_round2.md","description":"Read reciprocal-link and duplicates sections of reviews"}},"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CfffQqPfWPgdBJPRzyrj6","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"f29a1d57-f652-4427-9a70-348f20607f68","timestamp":"2026-10-03T17:01:44.626Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"f29a1d57-f652-4427-9a70-348f20607f68","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"bb7640f3-4c73-49eb-8121-41eedd8509ec","agentId":"a8e36db408996cf67","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018B9kewQ2NCJK2p591CzmBB","type":"tool_result","content":"1. PDF 270 / p.259: \"most weightlifting training is done with 1-3 repetitions for advanced weightlifters, thereby emphasizing the importance of 1RM, 2RM and 3RM loads ... especially where strength-skill is involved.\" Distinct from Vorobyev (1978) \"1-6\". Fold into the Berger entry (see its FIX).\n2. PDF 278 / p.267: the section's organising conclusion that perfecting speed-strength means combining two levers, \"the positive after-effect of preceding work\" and \"exercises in which muscular force opposes the inertia of the load and not its weight\", plus \"Thus, to develop speed-strength...\" summary. The pieces are in the loading entry and the arm-throw case, but neither states the two-lever summary. Optional one-sentence addition to `heuristic--speed-strength-loading-...` What it is.\n\n## Reciprocal-link suggestions\n\n- `heuristic--analytical-synthetic-and-multifaceted-preparation-suit-only-early-training` and `heuristic--choose-cross-training-by-fitness-qualities-and-phase-not-sport-label` (5.5).\n- `principle--speed-strength-strength-endurance-and-speed-endurance-are-independent-qualities` and `framework--four-principles-of-special-strength-training-organisation` (5.4).\n- `framework--three-methods-for-maximum-strength-repetitive-brief-maximal-isometric` and `heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size` (5.2.4).\n\n## Overall verdict\n\n**GO AFTER FIXES.** Required fixes:\n\n1. Newton II: flag ΔV as final velocity (equals change only from rest); \"net propulsive impulse\"; replace circular-motion example in (d) with constant-gravity projectile; \"net external force on the body\" in (c).\n2. Dynamic correspondence: fix \"paragraph before\" to \"paragraph that closes\" the dynamics discussion; label \"This passage is the authors' own statement ... Use it when\" as Inference.\n3. Three methods: say which load-rep pairing clashes (90-95%/3-6 clearly; 85-95%/3-5RM loosely); rewrite the line-105 fragment with an \"Eric's baseline:\" prefix.\n4. DeLorme: remove or label \"mostly in rehabilitation or untrained subjects\".\n5. Berger: correct the traceability sentence (conclusions 1 and 5 trace to Berger 1963 on p.259; only 6 is orphaned); add the 1-3 rep advanced-lifter sentence.\n6. Ivanov: retitle and rename (eccentric raised squat 1RM most; no \"own test\" for eccentric); label the \"trains the nervous system for that mode\" gloss.\n7. Implement mass: retitle and rename away from \"stay close to competition mass\"; fix When to apply; add the far-from-competition counterexamples (labelled Inference).\n8. After renames in 6 and 7, update inbound wikilinks in this batch.\n\nNo NO-GO. Both `contested` entries keep both sides on the cited pages. The single `proven` entry keeps its scope.\n----\n| principle--accelerating-specific-adaptation.md | FIX | Confidence. The entry rests `contested` on two definitions of the term and then says \"The two can coexist (reach the peak sooner with less volume).\" Ambiguous terminology is not a disagreement, and no other source or modern evidence is cited against the principle. As a Soviet-era prescription with no data on the page, the ceiling is `supported`. Quotes checked at PDF 307 / p.296 (image): definition, modelling, contingency plan, four means, statistical data, relative and starting strength claims; 20% sentence at PDF 305 (image). Baseline line 129 quoted faithfully (the baseline itself says \"Athletics Performance\"). Example labelled Inference. | Set `confidence: supported`. Change the heading \"**Contested inside the book (two definitions).**\" to \"**Two definitions in the book.**\" and keep the paragraph. |\n| principle--converging-partial-effects-of-strength-training-means.md | GO | Checked at PDF 305 (definition) and PDF 306 / p.295 (image): \"far apart\", \"firstly according to ability\", convergence sentences, criterion, role split. Fig 5.16 (image): Sport A starts highest, M below it, M1-M3 lower, all converge at the right. Bondarchuk inference matches the vault entry (narrow performance bands in most events, snatch exception in men's 110 m hurdles). Baseline line 102 checked. | None. |\n| principle--sequentially-introduce-means-of-greater-training-effect.md | FIX | Locator points to the wrong sentence. Entry: the ranking \"requires 'experimental assessment' (PDF p. 299 / printed p. 288).\" PDF 299's sentence is \"Experimental assessment of the cumulative effects of different variations of the combination of means is necessary,\" about combinations. The matching sentence is at PDF 308 / p.297: \"experimental assessment of the training effect and categorisation of the specific complex of means is necessary.\" All other quotes checked at PDF 302-303 (images): 5.3.2 opening, prerequisites, magnitude rule, two errors, repertoire, novelty, \"only once or twice\". Baseline line 102 checked. Example inference labelled. | Change to: \"which the authors say requires 'experimental assessment of the training effect and categorisation of the specific complex of means' (PDF p. 308 / printed p. 297).\" |\n\n## Duplicates\n\nNo entry restates an existing vault or staging entry without a new angle. Checked overlaps: the circuit claim vs Ch1 `claim--continuous-vs-interval-circuit-training-for-concurrent-goals` (Ch5 adds the CWT verdict and rest-interval mechanism, and links it); concentrated loading vs Bompa and Issurin entries (different author, linked); early specialisation vs Bompa LTAD and the Russian sport-school table (linked). Batch 5a/5b entries cover 5.1-5.2 and do not duplicate 5.3-5.6.\n\nReciprocal links to add (not blocking): novice case and sequence heuristic to `claim--depth-jump-training-beat-higher-volume-traditional-training-in-jumpers`; restoration heuristic to `claim--delayed-strength-training-effect-lag-before-gains-appear`.\n\n## Missing ideas (covered pages, not captured)\n\n1. PDF 299 / p.288: the \"mixed sessions\" regime is defined as strength and speed-strength sessions on the same day separated by a rest interval, with the authors crediting the after-effect and the \"contrasting of stimuli.\" No entry states what the regime actually was. (Folded into the novice-case fix above.)\n2. PDF 312 / p.301: one shared circuit can be individualised per client by contraction type, acceleration and velocity, posture, joint angle, hand and foot spacing. Not in any circuit entry; a short addition to the classification or progression entry would cover it.\n\nNothing else of weight is missing. The 5.3.1 principles of coupled effects and unity of physical and technical training are already quoted in the excess-volume claim; the ES-plus-plyometrics paragraph on PDF 301 is already atomized in the 4.2.1.6 electrostimulation entry.\n\n## Bompa finding (vault entry, out of scope for edits)\n\n`periodization--bompa/principle--concentrated-loading-overreaching` attributes to Siff and Verkhoshansky that gains \"may appear **4 to 12 weeks** after the concentrated block ends,\" and treats 4-12 weeks as the delay to the rebound. The vault entry reports its own source faithfully: Bompa & Haff 5e (PDF idx 61 of the Drive copy) says \"Siff and Verkhoshansky (83) suggested that performance gains may occur 4 to 12 weeks after the cessation of the concentrated loading phase.\" But against Supertraining 6e the attribution is wrong as a latency claim:\n- PDF 305 / p.294: gains after 4-8 week concentrated blocks \"may occur most commonly after a latency period of 1-3 weeks.\"\n- PDF 375 / p.364: the long-term delayed training effect \"was observed to last from 4-12 weeks\"; its duration T2 is \"approximately equal to the duration of the strength training stage (T1)\"; Fig 6.31 shows T2 beginning at the end of T1 with gains rising during T2.\n- PDF 381 / p.370 and 305: 4-12 weeks is the length of effective unidirectional training periods. PDF 536 / p.525: \"the tendency has been for the stages to last anywhere from 4-12 weeks.\"\n\nSo in Supertraining 4-12 weeks is a duration (of the block or of the delayed effect), never the wait before gains appear. The error originates in Bompa & Haff; the vault entry inherits it and sharpens it (\"the longer/larger the loading, the longer the delay\"). Caveat: reference 83 could not be read in the Drive copy (no reference list found; the figure credit on PDF 66 cites Siff 2003, Supertraining). The 6e pages checked here are consistent with the 1999 Siff & Verkhoshansky figures they reproduce. Suggested vault fix, for Eric: \"Bompa & Haff cite Siff and Verkhoshansky for gains 4 to 12 weeks after the block; Supertraining itself gives a 1-3 week latency and 4-12 weeks as the duration of the delayed effect (PDF 305, 375).\"\n\n## Overall verdict\n\n**GO AFTER FIXES.** 15 GO, 11 FIX, 0 NO-GO.\n\nRequired fixes:\n1. Deniskin: correct the Fig 5.14 Ss/Se description (both fall within stage 3; Se also ends below its stage-3 start).\n2. Junior sprinters: correct Fig 5.12 bar values (30 m LS combined about 0.25, 60 m LS 0.27 / 0.20 / 0.29, 100 m long about 0.48) and the sum comparisons; flag the 100 m edge bar as unclear.\n3. Novice sequencing: drop the mixed-sessions \"contradiction\"; add the PDF 288 session-order strand, the same-day definition of mixed sessions, and the link.\n4. Excess heavy strength: rewrite the \"volume, not intensity\" inference.\n5. Traditional circuit claim: label the 10-15 reps at over 80% judgment as Inference.\n6. Aerobic vs anaerobic circuits: narrow the contested strand to the anaerobic column vs its muscle-endurance sub-case; the PDF 317 scheme is a different target.\n7. Layout variants: label the superset-planning sentence as Inference.\n8. Allow restoration: place the 4-12 week misreading on Bompa & Haff's book, describe the Fig 6.31 timing, add the 4.1 delayed-lag link.\n9. Sequence jumps-barbell-depth: drop the mixed-sessions strand; add rule 1 vs PDF 299 and PDF 288; add the link.\n10. Accelerating specific adaptation: `contested` to `supported`; retitle the two-definitions note.\n11. Sequentially introduce means: move the \"experimental assessment\" locator from PDF 299 to PDF 308 / p.297.\n----\n| strength-endurance-ceiling | Yes for skaters 80-85%; no for rowers 50-80%. |\n| strength-endurance-hypertrophy | Yes, partly about scope; the same-page squat tension is stronger than the Ch4 one. |\n| high-volume-plyometrics | No. The conflict belongs to a neighbouring sentence. Change to `supported`. |\n| surface-footwear | No. Different aims, placed side by side on purpose. Change to `supported`. |\n\n## Duplicates\n\nNo duplicates found. I checked the vault supertraining folder, the staging folder (including batches 5a and 5c) and the vault research papers for depth jump, plyometric, strength-endurance, Lewis, inertia and state-dependent. The overlaps already link to the earlier entries: Ch1 shock-method terminology, Ch1.7 fibre-type prescription, Ch4.2.3 optimal drop height, and Ch3.2.2 strength-endurance taxonomy. Missing cross-batch link: the 5c entry `case-study--novice-strength-and-speed-strength-sequencing-experiments` should link both ways to `claim--depth-jump-training-beat-...` and `heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-...` (resistance-before-plyometrics rule).\n\n## Missing ideas (in the covered pages, not captured)\n\n1. The low-inertia vs high-inertia training effect (PDF 284 / p.273): with equal force from F = ma, \"Explosive, low inertia training targets involuntary, neuromuscular and central nervous processes more strongly than high inertia training, which has a greater effect on static strength development and muscle hypertrophy.\" Check first whether `principle--equal-force-different-loading-methods` (Ch1) already holds it.\n2. Strength-endurance specificity (PDF 296 / p.285): it \"is expressed to a lesser degree than the specificity of speed ... while its carry-over from one activity to another is greater.\"\n3. Fig 5.9 (PDF 285 / p.274, Verkhoshansky 1977): a catalogue of apparatus-based plyometric exercises for upper body, trunk and legs (drop push-ups between blocks, pulley-resisted kicks, incline sled). It is only cited for the range-of-motion sentence.\n4. Upper-body transfer of the depth-jump principles (PDF 288 / p.277): medicine ball catching and throwing, plyometric press-ups and bench presses, loaded swings. Only one line in the preparatory entry; relevant to linemen's hand work.\n5. \"Avoid the term plyometric drills\" (PDF 281 / p.270): drills suggest \"militarism and authoritarianism rather than individual flow\". Minor, voice-of-Siff coaching stance.\n\n## Overall verdict\n\n**GO AFTER FIXES.** Required fixes:\n1. high-volume: change `contested` to `supported`; point to the brief-maximal-tension entry for the conflict.\n2. surface-footwear: change `contested` to `supported`; rewrite the tension as different aims.\n3. strength-endurance-ceiling: drop rowers from the conflict; correct the glycolytic inference using baseline line 117.\n4. brief-maximal-tension: add the line-76 DSI tag verbatim; fix the \"jumping from depth\" Example; add the novice-sequencing caveat and link.\n5. 475-jumps: add the novice-sequencing caveat and link; title \"tons\".\n6. depth-jump-height: correct the mRSI baseline statement (readiness marker, line 74); add the Ch4 p.222 locator for \"considerably lower\".\n7. volume-and-rest: move item 1 and \"1-15 min\" out of the depth-jump model (Fig 5.11 shows ground jumps); label voices (Siff vs Verkhoshansky 1977).\n8. five-phases: soften the EM-delay \"conflict\" to a wording difference.\n9. classification and momentum-intensity: change the voice to Siff for the powermetrics passages.\n10. Lewis: take the Inference out of the title or label it; mark line 77 as Inferred.\n11. injury-risk: quote the PDF 281 margin-cut words as printed (\"adequ[ate]\", \"running a[nd] jumping\").\n12. resisted-water: quote the \"No resistance methods should not use\" double negative as printed.\n---\n\n1. **Upper-body transfer is already captured.** Justified. `heuristic--preparatory-progression-before-depth-jumps.md` line 23 holds the PDF 288 medicine-ball, press-up and bench-press sentence.\n2. **The novice caveat is scoped to the within-workout experiment.** Justified. PDF 299 has three findings: order \"has no real significance for novices\" across the preparatory period; the across-stage weights-then-depth-jumps variant \"was more effective\"; and the within-workout test found \"no statistically significant difference\". The fixer's wording is more accurate than round 1's. The one slip is the leftover word \"untested\" in the 475 entry (FIX above).\n3. **Item 1 is read as ground jumps by Inference, not fact.** Justified. \"Each exercise\" can grammatically take in the depth jumps, and Fig 5.11 shows no box.\n4. **Voice is labelled per item, not per model.** Justified. Items 4 and 6 sit in the PDF 285 prescription list. The PDF 286 list says \"Prolific experience\" and carries no citation; only its figures are credited to Verkhoshansky (1977).\n5. **The full line 77 tag is used.** Justified; it is verbatim.\n6. **Tags were added to every baseline citation in the edited files.** Every tag checked against method-baseline.md: 74 Explicit, 76 Explicit (Q21) plus its bracket tag, 77 Inferred (protocol; author uncertain), 105 Inferred (transcript + shared slides), 116 Explicit, 117 Explicit (Q19), 122 Explicit, 186 Explicit (Q15). All exact. The fixer did not extend this to the unedited files, which is why the weekly-frequency row is FIX.\n7. **Round-1 optional items for GO files were not applied.** Acceptable under the flagged-files-only rule. The line 105 tag in weekly frequency was more than cosmetic, so it is raised to FIX here.\n\n## Mechanical checks (all 30 files)\n\n- Wikilinks: 160 checked, all resolve in the staging library or the vault. None is wrapped across lines.\n- Section headers: all 30 files have each of the six headers exactly once, in the `**X** —` form.\n- Em dashes outside headers: 0. Titles: 0.\n- Review-history wording: 0. A keyword scan flagged 5 lines; all are ordinary prose (\"fixed function\", \"a corrected value\", \"not fixed by drop height\").\n- CR line endings: 0.\n\n## For the coordinator (outside scope, not a verdict item)\n\n- `case-study--novice-strength-and-speed-strength-sequencing-experiments.md` (5c) already links back to the 475-jumps entry. It does not link to `heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size`, which now cites it. Add that reciprocal link.\n\n## Overall verdict\n\n**GO AFTER FIXES.** Two one-line fixes:\n1. `claim--depth-jump-training-beat-higher-volume-traditional-training-in-jumpers.md`: change \"untested for novices\" to \"not supported for novices\".\n2. `heuristic--depth-jump-weekly-frequency-and-seasonal-placement.md`: tag the line 105 citation \"Inferred (transcript + shared slides); the baseline adds \\\"Not claimed in the interview\\\"\".\n\nAfter those two, the batch is ready to promote. Neither needs another full review round; a grep confirming both strings is enough.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"a8f0b570-5eb1-447b-953f-082952e82344","timestamp":"2026-10-03T17:01:44.832Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"1. PDF 270 / p.259: \"most weightlifting training is done with 1-3 repetitions for advanced weightlifters, thereby emphasizing the importance of 1RM, 2RM and 3RM loads ... especially where strength-skill is involved.\" Distinct from Vorobyev (1978) \"1-6\". Fold into the Berger entry (see its FIX).\n2. PDF 278 / p.267: the section's organising conclusion that perfecting speed-strength means combining two levers, \"the positive after-effect of preceding work\" and \"exercises in which muscular force opposes the inertia of the load and not its weight\", plus \"Thus, to develop speed-strength...\" summary. The pieces are in the loading entry and the arm-throw case, but neither states the two-lever summary. Optional one-sentence addition to `heuristic--speed-strength-loading-...` What it is.\n\n## Reciprocal-link suggestions\n\n- `heuristic--analytical-synthetic-and-multifaceted-preparation-suit-only-early-training` and `heuristic--choose-cross-training-by-fitness-qualities-and-phase-not-sport-label` (5.5).\n- `principle--speed-strength-strength-endurance-and-speed-endurance-are-independent-qualities` and `framework--four-principles-of-special-strength-training-organisation` (5.4).\n- `framework--three-methods-for-maximum-strength-repetitive-brief-maximal-isometric` and `heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size` (5.2.4).\n\n## Overall verdict\n\n**GO AFTER FIXES.** Required fixes:\n\n1. Newton II: flag ΔV as final velocity (equals change only from rest); \"net propulsive impulse\"; replace circular-motion example in (d) with constant-gravity projectile; \"net external force on the body\" in (c).\n2. Dynamic correspondence: fix \"paragraph before\" to \"paragraph that closes\" the dynamics discussion; label \"This passage is the authors' own statement ... Use it when\" as Inference.\n3. Three methods: say which load-rep pairing clashes (90-95%/3-6 clearly; 85-95%/3-5RM loosely); rewrite the line-105 fragment with an \"Eric's baseline:\" prefix.\n4. DeLorme: remove or label \"mostly in rehabilitation or untrained subjects\".\n5. Berger: correct the traceability sentence (conclusions 1 and 5 trace to Berger 1963 on p.259; only 6 is orphaned); add the 1-3 rep advanced-lifter sentence.\n6. Ivanov: retitle and rename (eccentric raised squat 1RM most; no \"own test\" for eccentric); label the \"trains the nervous system for that mode\" gloss.\n7. Implement mass: retitle and rename away from \"stay close to competition mass\"; fix When to apply; add the far-from-competition counterexamples (labelled Inference).\n8. After renames in 6 and 7, update inbound wikilinks in this batch.\n\nNo NO-GO. Both `contested` entries keep both sides on the cited pages. The single `proven` entry keeps its scope.\n----\n| principle--accelerating-specific-adaptation.md | FIX | Confidence. The entry rests `contested` on two definitions of the term and then says \"The two can coexist (reach the peak sooner with less volume).\" Ambiguous terminology is not a disagreement, and no other source or modern evidence is cited against the principle. As a Soviet-era prescription with no data on the page, the ceiling is `supported`. Quotes checked at PDF 307 / p.296 (image): definition, modelling, contingency plan, four means, statistical data, relative and starting strength claims; 20% sentence at PDF 305 (image). Baseline line 129 quoted faithfully (the baseline itself says \"Athletics Performance\"). Example labelled Inference. | Set `confidence: supported`. Change the heading \"**Contested inside the book (two definitions).**\" to \"**Two definitions in the book.**\" and keep the paragraph. |\n| principle--converging-partial-effects-of-strength-training-means.md | GO | Checked at PDF 305 (definition) and PDF 306 / p.295 (image): \"far apart\", \"firstly according to ability\", convergence sentences, criterion, role split. Fig 5.16 (image): Sport A starts highest, M below it, M1-M3 lower, all converge at the right. Bondarchuk inference matches the vault entry (narrow performance bands in most events, snatch exception in men's 110 m hurdles). Baseline line 102 checked. | None. |\n| principle--sequentially-introduce-means-of-greater-training-effect.md | FIX | Locator points to the wrong sentence. Entry: the ranking \"requires 'experimental assessment' (PDF p. 299 / printed p. 288).\" PDF 299's sentence is \"Experimental assessment of the cumulative effects of different variations of the combination of means is necessary,\" about combinations. The matching sentence is at PDF 308 / p.297: \"experimental assessment of the training effect and categorisation of the specific complex of means is necessary.\" All other quotes checked at PDF 302-303 (images): 5.3.2 opening, prerequisites, magnitude rule, two errors, repertoire, novelty, \"only once or twice\". Baseline line 102 checked. Example inference labelled. | Change to: \"which the authors say requires 'experimental assessment of the training effect and categorisation of the specific complex of means' (PDF p. 308 / printed p. 297).\" |\n\n## Duplicates\n\nNo entry restates an existing vault or staging entry without a new angle. Checked overlaps: the circuit claim vs Ch1 `claim--continuous-vs-interval-circuit-training-for-concurrent-goals` (Ch5 adds the CWT verdict and rest-interval mechanism, and links it); concentrated loading vs Bompa and Issurin entries (different author, linked); early specialisation vs Bompa LTAD and the Russian sport-school table (linked). Batch 5a/5b entries cover 5.1-5.2 and do not duplicate 5.3-5.6.\n\nReciprocal links to add (not blocking): novice case and sequence heuristic to `claim--depth-jump-training-beat-higher-volume-traditional-training-in-jumpers`; restoration heuristic to `claim--delayed-strength-training-effect-lag-before-gains-appear`.\n\n## Missing ideas (covered pages, not captured)\n\n1. PDF 299 / p.288: the \"mixed sessions\" regime is defined as strength and speed-strength sessions on the same day separated by a rest interval, with the authors crediting the after-effect and the \"contrasting of stimuli.\" No entry states what the regime actually was. (Folded into the novice-case fix above.)\n2. PDF 312 / p.301: one shared circuit can be individualised per client by contraction type, acceleration and velocity, posture, joint angle, hand and foot spacing. Not in any circuit entry; a short addition to the classification or progression entry would cover it.\n\nNothing else of weight is missing. The 5.3.1 principles of coupled effects and unity of physical and technical training are already quoted in the excess-volume claim; the ES-plus-plyometrics paragraph on PDF 301 is already atomized in the 4.2.1.6 electrostimulation entry.\n\n## Bompa finding (vault entry, out of scope for edits)\n\n`periodization--bompa/principle--concentrated-loading-overreaching` attributes to Siff and Verkhoshansky that gains \"may appear **4 to 12 weeks** after the concentrated block ends,\" and treats 4-12 weeks as the delay to the rebound. The vault entry reports its own source faithfully: Bompa & Haff 5e (PDF idx 61 of the Drive copy) says \"Siff and Verkhoshansky (83) suggested that performance gains may occur 4 to 12 weeks after the cessation of the concentrated loading phase.\" But against Supertraining 6e the attribution is wrong as a latency claim:\n- PDF 305 / p.294: gains after 4-8 week concentrated blocks \"may occur most commonly after a latency period of 1-3 weeks.\"\n- PDF 375 / p.364: the long-term delayed training effect \"was observed to last from 4-12 weeks\"; its duration T2 is \"approximately equal to the duration of the strength training stage (T1)\"; Fig 6.31 shows T2 beginning at the end of T1 with gains rising during T2.\n- PDF 381 / p.370 and 305: 4-12 weeks is the length of effective unidirectional training periods. PDF 536 / p.525: \"the tendency has been for the stages to last anywhere from 4-12 weeks.\"\n\nSo in Supertraining 4-12 weeks is a duration (of the block or of the delayed effect), never the wait before gains appear. The error originates in Bompa & Haff; the vault entry inherits it and sharpens it (\"the longer/larger the loading, the longer the delay\"). Caveat: reference 83 could not be read in the Drive copy (no reference list found; the figure credit on PDF 66 cites Siff 2003, Supertraining). The 6e pages checked here are consistent with the 1999 Siff & Verkhoshansky figures they reproduce. Suggested vault fix, for Eric: \"Bompa & Haff cite Siff and Verkhoshansky for gains 4 to 12 weeks after the block; Supertraining itself gives a 1-3 week latency and 4-12 weeks as the duration of the delayed effect (PDF 305, 375).\"\n\n## Overall verdict\n\n**GO AFTER FIXES.** 15 GO, 11 FIX, 0 NO-GO.\n\nRequired fixes:\n1. Deniskin: correct the Fig 5.14 Ss/Se description (both fall within stage 3; Se also ends below its stage-3 start).\n2. Junior sprinters: correct Fig 5.12 bar values (30 m LS combined about 0.25, 60 m LS 0.27 / 0.20 / 0.29, 100 m long about 0.48) and the sum comparisons; flag the 100 m edge bar as unclear.\n3. Novice sequencing: drop the mixed-sessions \"contradiction\"; add the PDF 288 session-order strand, the same-day definition of mixed sessions, and the link.\n4. Excess heavy strength: rewrite the \"volume, not intensity\" inference.\n5. Traditional circuit claim: label the 10-15 reps at over 80% judgment as Inference.\n6. Aerobic vs anaerobic circuits: narrow the contested strand to the anaerobic column vs its muscle-endurance sub-case; the PDF 317 scheme is a different target.\n7. Layout variants: label the superset-planning sentence as Inference.\n8. Allow restoration: place the 4-12 week misreading on Bompa & Haff's book, describe the Fig 6.31 timing, add the 4.1 delayed-lag link.\n9. Sequence jumps-barbell-depth: drop the mixed-sessions strand; add rule 1 vs PDF 299 and PDF 288; add the link.\n10. Accelerating specific adaptation: `contested` to `supported`; retitle the two-definitions note.\n11. Sequentially introduce means: move the \"experimental assessment\" locator from PDF 299 to PDF 308 / p.297.\n----\n| strength-endurance-ceiling | Yes for skaters 80-85%; no for rowers 50-80%. |\n| strength-endurance-hypertrophy | Yes, partly about scope; the same-page squat tension is stronger than the Ch4 one. |\n| high-volume-plyometrics | No. The conflict belongs to a neighbouring sentence. Change to `supported`. |\n| surface-footwear | No. Different aims, placed side by side on purpose. Change to `supported`. |\n\n## Duplicates\n\nNo duplicates found. I checked the vault supertraining folder, the staging folder (including batches 5a and 5c) and the vault research papers for depth jump, plyometric, strength-endurance, Lewis, inertia and state-dependent. The overlaps already link to the earlier entries: Ch1 shock-method terminology, Ch1.7 fibre-type prescription, Ch4.2.3 optimal drop height, and Ch3.2.2 strength-endurance taxonomy. Missing cross-batch link: the 5c entry `case-study--novice-strength-and-speed-strength-sequencing-experiments` should link both ways to `claim--depth-jump-training-beat-...` and `heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-...` (resistance-before-plyometrics rule).\n\n## Missing ideas (in the covered pages, not captured)\n\n1. The low-inertia vs high-inertia training effect (PDF 284 / p.273): with equal force from F = ma, \"Explosive, low inertia training targets involuntary, neuromuscular and central nervous processes more strongly than high inertia training, which has a greater effect on static strength development and muscle hypertrophy.\" Check first whether `principle--equal-force-different-loading-methods` (Ch1) already holds it.\n2. Strength-endurance specificity (PDF 296 / p.285): it \"is expressed to a lesser degree than the specificity of speed ... while its carry-over from one activity to another is greater.\"\n3. Fig 5.9 (PDF 285 / p.274, Verkhoshansky 1977): a catalogue of apparatus-based plyometric exercises for upper body, trunk and legs (drop push-ups between blocks, pulley-resisted kicks, incline sled). It is only cited for the range-of-motion sentence.\n4. Upper-body transfer of the depth-jump principles (PDF 288 / p.277): medicine ball catching and throwing, plyometric press-ups and bench presses, loaded swings. Only one line in the preparatory entry; relevant to linemen's hand work.\n5. \"Avoid the term plyometric drills\" (PDF 281 / p.270): drills suggest \"militarism and authoritarianism rather than individual flow\". Minor, voice-of-Siff coaching stance.\n\n## Overall verdict\n\n**GO AFTER FIXES.** Required fixes:\n1. high-volume: change `contested` to `supported`; point to the brief-maximal-tension entry for the conflict.\n2. surface-footwear: change `contested` to `supported`; rewrite the tension as different aims.\n3. strength-endurance-ceiling: drop rowers from the conflict; correct the glycolytic inference using baseline line 117.\n4. brief-maximal-tension: add the line-76 DSI tag verbatim; fix the \"jumping from depth\" Example; add the novice-sequencing caveat and link.\n5. 475-jumps: add the novice-sequencing caveat and link; title \"tons\".\n6. depth-jump-height: correct the mRSI baseline statement (readiness marker, line 74); add the Ch4 p.222 locator for \"considerably lower\".\n7. volume-and-rest: move item 1 and \"1-15 min\" out of the depth-jump model (Fig 5.11 shows ground jumps); label voices (Siff vs Verkhoshansky 1977).\n8. five-phases: soften the EM-delay \"conflict\" to a wording difference.\n9. classification and momentum-intensity: change the voice to Siff for the powermetrics passages.\n10. Lewis: take the Inference out of the title or label it; mark line 77 as Inferred.\n11. injury-risk: quote the PDF 281 margin-cut words as printed (\"adequ[ate]\", \"running a[nd] jumping\").\n12. resisted-water: quote the \"No resistance methods should not use\" double negative as printed.\n---\n\n1. **Upper-body transfer is already captured.** Justified. `heuristic--preparatory-progression-before-depth-jumps.md` line 23 holds the PDF 288 medicine-ball, press-up and bench-press sentence.\n2. **The novice caveat is scoped to the within-workout experiment.** Justified. PDF 299 has three findings: order \"has no real significance for novices\" across the preparatory period; the across-stage weights-then-depth-jumps variant \"was more effective\"; and the within-workout test found \"no statistically significant difference\". The fixer's wording is more accurate than round 1's. The one slip is the leftover word \"untested\" in the 475 entry (FIX above).\n3. **Item 1 is read as ground jumps by Inference, not fact.** Justified. \"Each exercise\" can grammatically take in the depth jumps, and Fig 5.11 shows no box.\n4. **Voice is labelled per item, not per model.** Justified. Items 4 and 6 sit in the PDF 285 prescription list. The PDF 286 list says \"Prolific experience\" and carries no citation; only its figures are credited to Verkhoshansky (1977).\n5. **The full line 77 tag is used.** Justified; it is verbatim.\n6. **Tags were added to every baseline citation in the edited files.** Every tag checked against method-baseline.md: 74 Explicit, 76 Explicit (Q21) plus its bracket tag, 77 Inferred (protocol; author uncertain), 105 Inferred (transcript + shared slides), 116 Explicit, 117 Explicit (Q19), 122 Explicit, 186 Explicit (Q15). All exact. The fixer did not extend this to the unedited files, which is why the weekly-frequency row is FIX.\n7. **Round-1 optional items for GO files were not applied.** Acceptable under the flagged-files-only rule. The line 105 tag in weekly frequency was more than cosmetic, so it is raised to FIX here.\n\n## Mechanical checks (all 30 files)\n\n- Wikilinks: 160 checked, all resolve in the staging library or the vault. None is wrapped across lines.\n- Section headers: all 30 files have each of the six headers exactly once, in the `**X** —` form.\n- Em dashes outside headers: 0. Titles: 0.\n- Review-history wording: 0. A keyword scan flagged 5 lines; all are ordinary prose (\"fixed function\", \"a corrected value\", \"not fixed by drop height\").\n- CR line endings: 0.\n\n## For the coordinator (outside scope, not a verdict item)\n\n- `case-study--novice-strength-and-speed-strength-sequencing-experiments.md` (5c) already links back to the 475-jumps entry. It does not link to `heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size`, which now cites it. Add that reciprocal link.\n\n## Overall verdict\n\n**GO AFTER FIXES.** Two one-line fixes:\n1. `claim--depth-jump-trainin