upertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--isokinetic-testing-limitations-for-functional-sport-specificity.md; echo '---'; sed -n 30,36p supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--four-mechanisms-of-stimulating-muscular-tension.md; echo '---'; sed -n 48,54p supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--kinesiological-pattern-and-strength-field-components.md; echo '---'; sed -n 54,60p supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--resistance-training-prescription-by-goal.md; echo '---'; sed -n 55,62p transfer-of-training-in-sports--bondarchuk/principle--conjugated-training-effects.md","description":"Inspect placeholder contexts"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CfffhBjidPCqPGN8nNVDg","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"36423941-ca51-454f-9176-0eb5ed85e9de","timestamp":"2026-10-03T17:05:27.793Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"36423941-ca51-454f-9176-0eb5ed85e9de","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"bb7640f3-4c73-49eb-8121-41eedd8509ec","agentId":"a8e36db408996cf67","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01F2L5eKZCGXq2B3wswVvvAe","type":"tool_result","content":"planetary-electron atomic model just because it is easy to work with; a more versatile,\n\"kinesiological polygraph\" combining high-speed video, EMG, myotensiometers, accelerometers\nand force plates is what multi-faceted human movement actually demands, and sporting\nperformance itself remains the ultimate test of any training or rehabilitation regime. The\nauthors also name the APRE system (based on DeLorme's original work, see 5.2.2) as an\ninexpensive, effective alternative to isokinetics, and point to Osternig (1986) for a\ndetailed review of isokinetic strengths and weaknesses. (PDF pp. 244-247, printed pp.\n233-236, \"4.2.6.2 Limitations of the Isokinetic Method,\" \"Functional Anatomy,\" \"Muscle\nPhysiology,\" \"The Importance of Specificity,\" \"Concluding Remarks.\")\n---\nVoluntary effort plays an important role in the first case, but its influence in the second\nis \"insignificant\"; the braking or amortisation mechanism causes a protective effector\nresponse more rapidly than a voluntary sequence of actions, and \"this mechanism forms the\nfoundation of plyometric training (5.2.4)\" (p.208; see\n[[framework--shock-method-and-plyometric-terminology]]). Inference: the four types map onto\nthe chapter's structure: type (4) as 4.2.1 Electrostimulation, type (1) as 4.2.2 Resistance\nand Strength Training, type (2) as 4.2.3 Kinetic Energy and Strength Processes, and type (3)\n---\n\n**When to apply** — Use the five-component strength field as a checklist when analyzing\nwhy a movement produces the force output it does, and to decide which special-strength\ntraining means (see later chapters) should target which component; the text itself only\nsays each component \"must be considered when ... selecting the special strength-training\nmeans\" (printed 107) without naming which method targets which component. **Inference:** a\nmovement that depends heavily on stored elastic energy (component 3) plausibly calls for\n---\nincomplete for advanced athletes: it ignores strength-speed, explosive strength,\nflexibility-strength, and strength-endurance as separate qualities (PDF p. 21, printed p.\n10; the \"average personal trainer\" quote is PDF p. 22, printed p. 11), and ignores the other\nprescription variables covered in\n[[heuristic--program-variable-complexity-scales-with-training-age]] and later chapters.\n**Inference:** do\nnot use this table as a complete program design tool for a well-trained collegiate\n---\nspecifically as coining \"dynamic correspondence,\" only \"other specialists\" with ref [157]\n(Menkhin) attached to the sentence. **Inference:** the course's Verkhoshansky attribution\nis the standard one in Western coaching literature; confirm against Supertraining's own\nChapter 4.3 (not yet ingested; see `_book.md` in the Supertraining staging folder) before\ntreating \"dynamic correspondence\" as a single-author concept in any future entry.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — **Confidence is supported, not proven.** This is Bondarchuk's own\nreading of Dyachkov's and Menkhin's proposals, cited only by bracketed","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"b00e004c-312c-4a89-9688-a5dff666855e","timestamp":"2026-10-03T17:05:28.005Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"planetary-electron atomic model just because it is easy to work with; a more versatile,\n\"kinesiological polygraph\" combining high-speed video, EMG, myotensiometers, accelerometers\nand force plates is what multi-faceted human movement actually demands, and sporting\nperformance itself remains the ultimate test of any training or rehabilitation regime. The\nauthors also name the APRE system (based on DeLorme's original work, see 5.2.2) as an\ninexpensive, effective alternative to isokinetics, and point to Osternig (1986) for a\ndetailed review of isokinetic strengths and weaknesses. (PDF pp. 244-247, printed pp.\n233-236, \"4.2.6.2 Limitations of the Isokinetic Method,\" \"Functional Anatomy,\" \"Muscle\nPhysiology,\" \"The Importance of Specificity,\" \"Concluding Remarks.\")\n---\nVoluntary effort plays an important role in the first case, but its influence in the second\nis \"insignificant\"; the braking or amortisation mechanism causes a protective effector\nresponse more rapidly than a voluntary sequence of actions, and \"this mechanism forms the\nfoundation of plyometric training (5.2.4)\" (p.208; see\n[[framework--shock-method-and-plyometric-terminology]]). Inference: the four types map onto\nthe chapter's structure: type (4) as 4.2.1 Electrostimulation, type (1) as 4.2.2 Resistance\nand Strength Training, type (2) as 4.2.3 Kinetic Energy and Strength Processes, and type (3)\n---\n\n**When to apply** — Use the five-component strength field as a checklist when analyzing\nwhy a movement produces the force output it does, and to decide which special-strength\ntraining means (see later chapters) should target which component; the text itself only\nsays each component \"must be considered when ... selecting the special strength-training\nmeans\" (printed 107) without naming which method targets which component. **Inference:** a\nmovement that depends heavily on stored elastic energy (component 3) plausibly calls for\n---\nincomplete for advanced athletes: it ignores strength-speed, explosive strength,\nflexibility-strength, and strength-endurance as separate qualities (PDF p. 21, printed p.\n10; the \"average personal trainer\" quote is PDF p. 22, printed p. 11), and ignores the other\nprescription variables covered in\n[[heuristic--program-variable-complexity-scales-with-training-age]] and later chapters.\n**Inference:** do\nnot use this table as a complete program design tool for a well-trained collegiate\n---\nspecifically as coining \"dynamic correspondence,\" only \"other specialists\" with ref [157]\n(Menkhin) attached to the sentence. **Inference:** the course's Verkhoshansky attribution\nis the standard one in Western coaching literature; confirm against Supertraining's own\nChapter 4.3 (not yet ingested; see `_book.md` in the Supertraining staging folder) before\ntreating \"dynamic correspondence\" as a single-author concept in any future entry.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — **Confidence is supported, not proven.** This is Bondarchuk's own\nreading of Dyachkov's and Menkhin's proposals, cited only by bracketed","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"36423941-ca51-454f-9176-0eb5ed85e9de","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
