{"parentUuid":"5b002292-da2b-46cc-83bf-936e61e12ea7","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f3859be2-3513-4b8e-bb88-ac5e3cefe2da","agentId":"a8e36db408996cf67","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01K3gwTbC6LVppNJMbJ7wvpY","type":"tool_result","content":"35:**Related** — [[framework--isometric-push-hold-switch-catch-continuum]] — the four exercise types run in these positions.\n36:- Added 2026-09-26: [[heuristic--75-85-percent-mv-decision-gate]]: an RTP application of isometric force benchmarks (Nordic and seated calf) as a gate before faster running.\n- **Knee push:** bar in a high back-squat position; feet hip-width apart.\n- **Hip push:** a bridge with the bar across the hip crease, and three contact points: the lower shoulder blade on a softer box, the heel on a hard box or force plate, and the knee slightly flexed (about 40° or straighter). Keep the heel dorsiflexed and the body horizontally \"stacked.\"\n- **All three:** keep the swing leg in front of the stance leg for specificity, although he is \"not too anal\" about knee height.\n\n**When to apply** — Use this when building any unilateral isometric test or training station. In football the knee and ankle stations are easy to add to a ForceDecks testing day. A single-leg iso squat at 35–40° knee flexion is a reasonable, repeatable position to track across a calf or quad RTP and back to baseline.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The angles describe **upright** sprint mid-stance. Acceleration stance is more flexed, so these angles are not specific to the first steps. None of the three positions targets the **hamstrings as knee flexors** or at long length; the hip bridge is the nearest, and it is still a hip-extension task. Do not treat these positions as hamstring RTP tests. Natera accepts about ±5° error, so the angles are not precise targets. Hip ratios are expressed against **\"system weight\"**, the ~33% of body mass supported in the bridge, not against body weight. Mix the two conventions and hip numbers will not be comparable across athletes or with other sources.\n\n**Example** — Ankle station: Smith bar on the shoulders, the athlete stands tall on one leg on a ForceDecks plate with the knee locked, rises to about 5° plantar flexion, and **pushes to max while the coach sets the bar stops**. That removes soft-tissue slack, so the position is set under load and not at rest.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--isometric-push-hold-switch-catch-continuum]] — the four exercise types run in these positions.\n- Added 2026-09-26: [[heuristic--75-85-percent-mv-decision-gate]]: an RTP application of isometric force benchmarks (Nordic and seated calf) as a gate before faster running.\n---TEXAS\n      63 strength-and-conditioning/velocity-based-training-implementation-guide-from-texas-am.md\n11:# Velocity-Based Training Implementation Guide from Texas A&M\n19:## Key topics\n24:- **Training integration**: Real-time feedback via [[catapult-perch-integration-athlete-monitoring|Perch]] system enhances coaching and athlete engagement\n30:## Decisions\n36:## Action items\n43:## Open questions\n51:## Meeting notes (condensed)\n---\ntype: meeting\ncreated: 2025-08-21\nsource: \"Fireflies meeting transcript\"\ndomain: [strength-and-conditioning, velocity-based-training, coaching]\ntags: [vbt, perch-system, load-management, athlete-assessment, performance-monitoring, autoregulation, texas-am]\nstatus: ingested\ncross_linked: true\n---\n\n# Velocity-Based Training Implementation Guide from Texas A&M\n\n> **Summary**\n> **Source:** Fireflies meeting · **Date:** 2025-08-21 · **Duration:** 73 minutes\n> **Key people:** Jeremy Jacobs (Texas A&M), Eric Rash\n>\n> Jeremy Jacobs from Texas A&M shared practical application of Velocity Based Training (VBT) for high school and college coaches. The program integrates VBT within Olympic lifts without altering existing methodologies, using velocity data to inform coaching decisions. VBT helps manage roster turnover through rapid athlete assessments and enables daily autoregulation of load based on individual readiness.\n\n## Key topics","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"3ef36533-4966-4942-a71f-c4f507bc2596","timestamp":"2026-10-03T17:05:50.783Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"35:**Related** — [[framework--isometric-push-hold-switch-catch-continuum]] — the four exercise types run in these positions.\n36:- Added 2026-09-26: [[heuristic--75-85-percent-mv-decision-gate]]: an RTP application of isometric force benchmarks (Nordic and seated calf) as a gate before faster running.\n- **Knee push:** bar in a high back-squat position; feet hip-width apart.\n- **Hip push:** a bridge with the bar across the hip crease, and three contact points: the lower shoulder blade on a softer box, the heel on a hard box or force plate, and the knee slightly flexed (about 40° or straighter). Keep the heel dorsiflexed and the body horizontally \"stacked.\"\n- **All three:** keep the swing leg in front of the stance leg for specificity, although he is \"not too anal\" about knee height.\n\n**When to apply** — Use this when building any unilateral isometric test or training station. In football the knee and ankle stations are easy to add to a ForceDecks testing day. A single-leg iso squat at 35–40° knee flexion is a reasonable, repeatable position to track across a calf or quad RTP and back to baseline.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The angles describe **upright** sprint mid-stance. Acceleration stance is more flexed, so these angles are not specific to the first steps. None of the three positions targets the **hamstrings as knee flexors** or at long length; the hip bridge is the nearest, and it is still a hip-extension task. Do not treat these positions as hamstring RTP tests. Natera accepts about ±5° error, so the angles are not precise targets. Hip ratios are expressed against **\"system weight\"**, the ~33% of body mass supported in the bridge, not against body weight. Mix the two conventions and hip numbers will not be comparable across athletes or with other sources.\n\n**Example** — Ankle station: Smith bar on the shoulders, the athlete stands tall on one leg on a ForceDecks plate with the knee locked, rises to about 5° plantar flexion, and **pushes to max while the coach sets the bar stops**. That removes soft-tissue slack, so the position is set under load and not at rest.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--isometric-push-hold-switch-catch-continuum]] — the four exercise types run in these positions.\n- Added 2026-09-26: [[heuristic--75-85-percent-mv-decision-gate]]: an RTP application of isometric force benchmarks (Nordic and seated calf) as a gate before faster running.\n---TEXAS\n      63 strength-and-conditioning/velocity-based-training-implementation-guide-from-texas-am.md\n11:# Velocity-Based Training Implementation Guide from Texas A&M\n19:## Key topics\n24:- **Training integration**: Real-time feedback via [[catapult-perch-integration-athlete-monitoring|Perch]] system enhances coaching and athlete engagement\n30:## Decisions\n36:## Action items\n43:## Open questions\n51:## Meeting notes (condensed)\n---\ntype: meeting\ncreated: 2025-08-21\nsource: \"Fireflies meeting transcript\"\ndomain: [strength-and-conditioning, velocity-based-training, coaching]\ntags: [vbt, perch-system, load-management, athlete-assessment, performance-monitoring, autoregulation, texas-am]\nstatus: ingested\ncross_linked: true\n---\n\n# Velocity-Based Training Implementation Guide from Texas A&M\n\n> **Summary**\n> **Source:** Fireflies meeting · **Date:** 2025-08-21 · **Duration:** 73 minutes\n> **Key people:** Jeremy Jacobs (Texas A&M), Eric Rash\n>\n> Jeremy Jacobs from Texas A&M shared practical application of Velocity Based Training (VBT) for high school and college coaches. The program integrates VBT within Olympic lifts without altering existing methodologies, using velocity data to inform coaching decisions. VBT helps manage roster turnover through rapid athlete assessments and enables daily autoregulation of load based on individual readiness.\n\n## Key topics","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"5b002292-da2b-46cc-83bf-936e61e12ea7","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
