{"parentUuid":"dc6e551f-a51f-485e-83c0-67480364f842","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a8e36db408996cf67","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfffjAYLeAFyoM1r6AmKM","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1952,"cache_read_input_tokens":129031,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1952,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":5161,"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CfffjA6oZnmwSiK83573x","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"8abb2333-1105-42a5-be1e-899bda52579f","timestamp":"2026-10-03T17:05:57.453Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"8abb2333-1105-42a5-be1e-899bda52579f","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a8e36db408996cf67","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfffjAYLeAFyoM1r6AmKM","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01LvzcPhaB9VrrBaMa6123h7","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library && sed -n 19,63p strength-and-conditioning/velocity-based-training-implementation-guide-from-texas-am.md; ls strength-and-conditioning/*.md | head; grep -rln 'velocity-based-training-implementation-guide-from-texas-am' . | head","description":"Read Texas A&M note body and inbound links"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1952,"cache_read_input_tokens":129031,"output_tokens":788,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":586},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1952},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":788,"cache_read_input_tokens":129031,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1952,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1952,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01LvzcPhaB9VrrBaMa6123h7":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library && sed -n 19,63p strength-and-conditioning/velocity-based-training-implementation-guide-from-texas-am.md; ls strength-and-conditioning/*.md | head; grep -rln 'velocity-based-training-implementation-guide-from-texas-am' . | head","description":"Read Texas A&M note body and inbound links"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CfffjA6oZnmwSiK83573x","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"62434084-fec5-44f5-bbb1-30cd642a42da","timestamp":"2026-10-03T17:05:58.740Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"62434084-fec5-44f5-bbb1-30cd642a42da","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f3859be2-3513-4b8e-bb88-ac5e3cefe2da","agentId":"a8e36db408996cf67","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LvzcPhaB9VrrBaMa6123h7","type":"tool_result","content":"## Key topics\n\n- **Historical context**: VBT evolution from Verkoshansky's stopwatch usage to modern bar speed tracking methodologies\n- **Velocity zones**: Defined thresholds for different training adaptations (strength, power, hypertrophy)\n- **Safety protocols**: Minimum velocity thresholds prevent injuries (e.g., 0.3 m/s back squat, 1.0 m/s power clean)\n- **Training integration**: Real-time feedback via [[catapult-perch-integration-athlete-monitoring|Perch]] system enhances coaching and athlete engagement\n- **Data tracking**: Mean velocity and load-velocity profiles inform programming without extensive testing\n- **Athlete assessment**: Rapid profiling aids in tailoring training during high roster turnover\n- **Daily monitoring**: Velocity autoregulation fosters responsiveness to individual readiness levels\n- **Multi-metric integration**: GPS and jump tracking data enhance comprehensive athlete profiling\n\n## Decisions\n\n- Implement basic VBT principles without modifying existing programs initially\n- Establish minimum velocity safety thresholds by lift type and training age\n- Set up athlete profiles with current 1RM data before implementing velocity-based programming\n\n## Action items\n\n- Implement basic VBT principles starting with existing programs without modifications to assess current training effectiveness\n- Begin daily data collection and export rather than bulk analysis for easier management\n- Establish minimum velocity thresholds for safety in back squat (0.3 m/s), bench press, and power clean (1.0 m/s) exercises\n- Set up athlete profiles with current 1RM data before implementing velocity-based programming\n\n## Open questions\n\n- What is the optimal frequency for velocity assessment across different training phases?\n- How should velocity data inform load management decisions for injured or returning athletes?\n- What integration pathway exists between Perch VBT system and other performance monitoring tools?\n\n---\n\n## Meeting notes (condensed)\n\nJeremy Jacobs presented VBT as a practical coaching tool at Texas A&M. The program emphasizes that coaches can integrate velocity feedback without dismantling existing strength programs. Velocity data serves three primary functions: safety monitoring via minimum thresholds, athlete assessment during roster transitions, and daily autoregulation of training loads.\n\nThe historical perspective traces VBT from Verkoshansky's early work through modern implementations. Velocity zones establish clear markers for different training adaptations—strength zones differ from power or hypertrophy zones—creating a structured framework for programming decisions.\n\nSafety is paramount. Setting minimum velocity thresholds (0.3 m/s for back squat, 1.0 m/s for power clean) prevents athletes from lifting with compromised form or excessive fatigue. These thresholds vary by training age, protecting newer athletes more conservatively.\n\nReal-time feedback systems like Perch provide immediate coaching cues on bar path and movement quality, enhancing athlete engagement and coaching precision. Rather than relying on periodic testing, daily velocity monitoring reveals individual load-velocity profiles and performance trends.\n\nDuring roster turnover, velocity testing rapidly establishes athlete profiles and informs individualized programming. This is particularly valuable in college settings where fresh cohorts arrive annually.\n\nThe implementation strategy starts simple: collect daily velocity data and export it regularly rather than attempting complex bulk analysis. Once data collection is normalized, programming decisions become more sophisticated, informed by actual performance metrics rather than assumptions.\nstrength-and-conditioning/_domain.md\nstrength-and-conditioning/velocity-based-training-implementation-guide-from-texas-am.md\nstrength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--apre-autoregulating-progressive-resistance-exercise.md","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"18a7253f-8095-4d4c-8dcf-5fc0fb40947a","timestamp":"2026-10-03T17:05:59.444Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"## Key topics\n\n- **Historical context**: VBT evolution from Verkoshansky's stopwatch usage to modern bar speed tracking methodologies\n- **Velocity zones**: Defined thresholds for different training adaptations (strength, power, hypertrophy)\n- **Safety protocols**: Minimum velocity thresholds prevent injuries (e.g., 0.3 m/s back squat, 1.0 m/s power clean)\n- **Training integration**: Real-time feedback via [[catapult-perch-integration-athlete-monitoring|Perch]] system enhances coaching and athlete engagement\n- **Data tracking**: Mean velocity and load-velocity profiles inform programming without extensive testing\n- **Athlete assessment**: Rapid profiling aids in tailoring training during high roster turnover\n- **Daily monitoring**: Velocity autoregulation fosters responsiveness to individual readiness levels\n- **Multi-metric integration**: GPS and jump tracking data enhance comprehensive athlete profiling\n\n## Decisions\n\n- Implement basic VBT principles without modifying existing programs initially\n- Establish minimum velocity safety thresholds by lift type and training age\n- Set up athlete profiles with current 1RM data before implementing velocity-based programming\n\n## Action items\n\n- Implement basic VBT principles starting with existing programs without modifications to assess current training effectiveness\n- Begin daily data collection and export rather than bulk analysis for easier management\n- Establish minimum velocity thresholds for safety in back squat (0.3 m/s), bench press, and power clean (1.0 m/s) exercises\n- Set up athlete profiles with current 1RM data before implementing velocity-based programming\n\n## Open questions\n\n- What is the optimal frequency for velocity assessment across different training phases?\n- How should velocity data inform load management decisions for injured or returning athletes?\n- What integration pathway exists between Perch VBT system and other performance monitoring tools?\n\n---\n\n## Meeting notes (condensed)\n\nJeremy Jacobs presented VBT as a practical coaching tool at Texas A&M. The program emphasizes that coaches can integrate velocity feedback without dismantling existing strength programs. Velocity data serves three primary functions: safety monitoring via minimum thresholds, athlete assessment during roster transitions, and daily autoregulation of training loads.\n\nThe historical perspective traces VBT from Verkoshansky's early work through modern implementations. Velocity zones establish clear markers for different training adaptations—strength zones differ from power or hypertrophy zones—creating a structured framework for programming decisions.\n\nSafety is paramount. Setting minimum velocity thresholds (0.3 m/s for back squat, 1.0 m/s for power clean) prevents athletes from lifting with compromised form or excessive fatigue. These thresholds vary by training age, protecting newer athletes more conservatively.\n\nReal-time feedback systems like Perch provide immediate coaching cues on bar path and movement quality, enhancing athlete engagement and coaching precision. Rather than relying on periodic testing, daily velocity monitoring reveals individual load-velocity profiles and performance trends.\n\nDuring roster turnover, velocity testing rapidly establishes athlete profiles and informs individualized programming. This is particularly valuable in college settings where fresh cohorts arrive annually.\n\nThe implementation strategy starts simple: collect daily velocity data and export it regularly rather than attempting complex bulk analysis. Once data collection is normalized, programming decisions become more sophisticated, informed by actual performance metrics rather than assumptions.\nstrength-and-conditioning/_domain.md\nstrength-and-conditioning/velocity-based-training-implementation-guide-from-texas-am.md\nstrength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--apre-autoregulating-progressive-resistance-exercise.md","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"62434084-fec5-44f5-bbb1-30cd642a42da","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
