{"parentUuid":"b3ff9151-9347-412a-9407-a481b9a5e492","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a8e36db408996cf67","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cfffm85KMyk7Avh1CrBEM","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_015JPgrBN74t7mruqzSKqPft","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/projects/-Users-kristinarash-DEV/9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c/tool-results/biq0w8b0l.txt"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1133,"cache_read_input_tokens":135129,"output_tokens":122,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1133},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":122,"cache_read_input_tokens":135129,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1133,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1133,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_015JPgrBN74t7mruqzSKqPft":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/projects/-Users-kristinarash-DEV/9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c/tool-results/biq0w8b0l.txt"}},"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011Cfffm7ownuFF53q6cxxB2","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"7f8742b4-2e77-4a14-af2e-f734023be81c","timestamp":"2026-10-03T17:06:19.296Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"7f8742b4-2e77-4a14-af2e-f734023be81c","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f3859be2-3513-4b8e-bb88-ac5e3cefe2da","agentId":"a8e36db408996cf67","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015JPgrBN74t7mruqzSKqPft","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t### case-study--alternate-leg-bounding-with-thigh-cuffs-for-sprinters\n2\tchapter: \"5.2.3\"\n3\tconfidence: untested\n4\t**What it is** — \"In certain cases, power can be increased without using additional loads. For example, with jumps from one leg to the other, the push-off displays values of power which are greater than in running. Therefore, these jumps are excellent for the special strength training of sprinters.\" They must be done \"by not accentuating the push-off of the body (which does not happen in sprinting), but by active gathering-in of the planted leg (i.e. via hip flexion).\" \"These jumps should be executed repetitively over distances of 50-100 meters as fast as possible. The addition of small cuffs weighing from 100-150 grams on each thigh can makes such exercises more effective in experienced ath\n5\t\n6\t### case-study--arm-throw-height-after-barbell-press-tonic-work\n7\tchapter: \"5.2.3\"\n8\tconfidence: untested\n9\t**What it is** — \"The progressive development of speed-strength is the result of combining means for exploiting the after-effect phenomenon of preceding work to increase the effectiveness of subsequent work.\" In an \"exercise modelling\" experiment, \"the working effect of an explosive movement, as measured by the height to which a load was thrown (Fig 5.4), increases by an average of 38-40% after execution of barbell presses for 3 sets of 3 repetitions with 80% of the 1RM (with a rest interval of 10 minutes between the pressing and the throwing),\" with shorter movement time and greater working path, average speed, driving force and power (PDF p. 276 / printed p. 265). Table 5.3, \"Arm movement \n10\t\n11\t### case-study--deniskin-depth-jumps-after-resistance-and-rest-as-a-training-means\n12\tchapter: \"5.3\"\n13\tconfidence: supported\n14\t**What it is** — \"qualified athletes modelled different variants of weightlifting pre-competition training under laboratory conditions (Deniskin, 1976).\" The test movement was \"A leg extension movement involving the lifting of a 1RM ... starting with an initial knee angle of 90°.\" Stage 1 was \"intensive work with resistance, the purpose of which was to increase explosive strength.\" \"Only depth-jumps were used during the second and third stages.\" Two depth-jump stages were used because \"a preliminary investigation ... showed that the rise in functional state achieved after one stage is unstable.\" \"The fourth stage served as a control and served only to test performance.\" \"The subjects were gi\n15\t\n16\t### case-study--novice-strength-and-speed-strength-sequencing-experiments\n17\tchapter: \"5.3\"\n18\tconfidence: contested\n19\t**What it is** — The authors report a set of experiments on novices in speed-strength sports (researchers named as \"Purvin, Khodykin, Deniskin, Tatian and Chernousov\", names checked against the page image; no dates, sample sizes or statistics are given) (PDF p. 299 / printed p. 288): 1. **Order across the preparatory period.** \"the sequence of means in the preparatory period (beginning with strength, then speed-strength means, or vice-versa) has no real significance for novices. Consequently, the same effect can be obtained by using these means in any order.\" 2. **Mixed sessions.** \"A relatively larger effect than produced by the above-mentioned variants (with the same volume of training loa\n20\t\n21\t### claim--depth-jump-training-beat-higher-volume-traditional-training-in-jumpers\n22\tchapter: \"5.2.4\"\n23\tconfidence: supported\n24\t**What it is** — \"The plyometric method of developing explosive strength and reactive ability solves to a significant degree the problem of economising training, since it ensures that a high level of special conditioning is attained in the minimum time. Thus, a comparative experiment revealed that a group of track-and-field jumpers executing primarily depth-jumps (all of them did 475 jumps) over a 12 week period in the preparation phase showed greater improvement in reactive ability than the group which trained with traditional methods and executed 1472 general push-offs (squats, jumping and hopping with a barbell weighing 90-95%, 70-80% and 30-40% of 1RM) and lifted a total volume of 93 ton\n25\t\n26\t### claim--excess-heavy-strength-volume-impairs-speed-strength-and-technique\n27\tchapter: \"5.3.1\"\n28\tconfidence: supported\n29\t**What it is** — The authors grant that general heavy resistance exercises \"enable the athlete to reach the necessary level of absolute strength, to provide general physical conditioning and to train the neuromuscular system non-specifically,\" but \"it is undesirable to use them extensively during specialised training phases, since they can diminish speed and explosiveness. High volume strength loads used after a phase of specialised jump training can substantially reduce the existing level of explosive strength (by as much as 30%)\" (PDF p. 302 / printed p. 291; all figures in this entry checked against the page image). Supporting reports, all cited without dates except Deniskin: Kotz, \"prolo\n30\t\n31\t### claim--heavy-squats-and-light-fast-lifts-miss-the-eccentric-concentric-switch\n32\tchapter: \"5.2.4\"\n33\tconfidence: supported\n34\t**What it is** — The authors open 5.2.4 by analysing the traditional speed-strength means. In heavy barbell squats \"the muscles work very slowly (quasi-isometrically) with a relatively constant tension produced by the load. Consequently, the muscles primarily develop isometric strength\". Raising the barbell weight, \"frequently considered a basic indicator of special strength fitness, places a large and generally unnecessary load on the spine.\" For speed-strength, athletes use smaller loads; their example is \"squat thrusts with a 60kg barbell on the shoulders\", which \"produces a large dynamic maximum force\". So \"exercises with heavy weights tend to increase the strength potential of the muscl\n35\t\n36\t### claim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-keep-technique-and-size-per-athlete\n37\tchapter: \"5.2.3\"\n38\tconfidence: supported\n39\t**What it is** — For acyclic speed-strength the authors say \"plyometric or throwing exercises should be selected on the basis of their relevance to the sports activity,\" and give a run of implement findings (PDF p. 276-277 / printed p. 265-266, verified against the page images). Water polo throw: a 2 kg medicine ball beat a 4 kg ball, throwing distance up \"13.6% and 8.9%, respectively,\" and \"throwing a 4 kg ball is detrimental to technique (Rogener, 1961).\" Baseball: a light \"57 grams\" and heavy \"184 gm\" ball both improved throwing (regulation \"about 148 gm\"), but \"throwing the light ball improves precision for throwing the heavier ball, but the reverse effect does not occur (Egstrom et al, \n40\t\n41\t### claim--ivanov-1966-contraction-mode-and-which-strength-test-improves\n42\tchapter: \"5.2.1\"\n43\tconfidence: supported\n44\t**What it is** — In \"A 3-month experiment comparing the effectiveness of concentric, eccentric and isometric work,\" \"the largest increase in a 1RM squat occurred with eccentric work (average 15 kg), and the smallest with isometric tension (9.2 kg).\" \"Not unexpectedly, the largest increase in isometric strength was obtained with isometric tension (30.2 kg), and the smallest increase with concentric work (14.6 kg).\" \"Vertical jump increased only with the concentric training regime (3.7 cm)\"; it \"decreased by 1.6 cm for the eccentric regime and by 5.4 cm for isometric tension (Ivanov, 1966)\" (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261, numbers verified against the page image; the text layer misreads 30.2 as \"\n45\t\n46\t### claim--moderate-and-mixed-movement-tempo-for-repetition-strength-gains\n47\tchapter: \"5.2.1\"\n48\tconfidence: supported\n49\t**What it is** — The authors report that \"The movement tempo of resistance exercises also has great significance for the development of muscular strength (Vorobyev, 1978).\" With a moderate tempo \"strength continues to increase over a period of 30 training sessions, whereas it increases only over a period of 15 sessions with other tempos,\" and \"the most effective variant is a combination of different movement tempos\": over 10 weeks the gain \"was 22.6 ± 0.6 kg; and 16.3 ± 0.5 kg for the execution of movements at a moderate tempo for both concentric and eccentric actions (Lelikov, 1975)\" (PDF p. 271 / printed p. 260, verified against the page image). In Vasiliev (1954), after 40 sessions of equ\n50\t\n51\t### claim--newton-ii-and-impulse-momentum-as-the-math-behind-the-force-time-curve\n52\tchapter: \"5.1\"\n53\tconfidence: proven\n54\t**What it is** — The authors open Chapter 5 by saying that if \"the Force-Time curve is to be regarded as the graphical starting point for sport biomechanics, then Newton's 2nd Law of Motion must logically serve as the corresponding mathematical starting point\" (PDF p. 266 / printed p. 255). They state Newton II as \"Force is proportional to the rate of change of momentum,\" write it as equation (5.1), F = d(m.v)/dt = m.dv/dt + v.dm/dt, and reduce it to F = m.a \"if the mass of the system is not changing with time,\" which they say holds in sport (PDF p. 266-267 / printed p. 255-256; equations verified against the page image). They then separate force \"generated ballistically or impulsively\" from\n55\t\n56\t### claim--plyometric-skill-is-cognitive-and-state-dependent\n57\tchapter: \"5.2.4\"\n58\tconfidence: untested\n59\t**What it is** — Under \"Plyometrics and the Brain\" (PDF pp. 294-295 / printed pp. 283-284, text layer): - \"plyometric action is not simply a phenomenon that can be adequately explained on the basis of stored elastic energy and the stretch shortening cycle.\" Although the action itself is fast and spinal, \"the preparatory period prior to the plyometric action can involve higher order mental processing of exceptional intricacy.\" - **Cognitive versus non-cognitive plyometrics:** a plyometric action \"under conditions in which the athlete anticipates a particular course of action (*cognitive plyometrics*)\" versus \"a purely reflexive response under unexpected circumstances (*non-cognitive plyometri\n60\t\n61\t### claim--prestretch-benefit-decay-time-differs-by-task\n62\tchapter: \"5.2.4\"\n63\tconfidence: contested\n64\t**What it is** — After stating the about 0.15 s coupling guide, the authors say it \"requires some qualification\" and list conflicting results (PDF pp. 281-282 / printed pp. 270-271; Figs 5.6 and 5.7 verified against the page image): - Wilson et al (1990), bench press: \"the benefits of prior stretch may endure for as long as 4 seconds, at which stage it is suggested that all stored elastic energy is lost (see Fig 5.6).\" - Chapman and Caldwell (1985), forearm movement: benefits \"dissipated within 0.25 second, a figure which agrees with Siff's analysis of explosive rebound elbow flexion without additional loading (Fig 5.7).\" - Wilson et al (1991), rebound action of the chest and arms: \"no benef\n65\t\n66\t### claim--strength-endurance-hypertrophy-and-cyclic-endurance\n67\tchapter: \"5.2.5\"\n68\tconfidence: contested\n69\t**What it is** — From 5.2.5 (PDF pp. 297-298 / printed pp. 286-287, verified against the page images): - \"an increase in strength-endurance contributes to improving endurance in distance runners, skiers and skaters (Popov, 1968; Nabatnikova, 1972; Mikhailov & Panov, 1975). However ... the magnitude of the strength load used is different if, on the one hand, the key quality required is general endurance and, on the other hand, it is strength-endurance.\" - \"certain popular strength exercises such as running with a sack of sand weighing 25% of the subject's bodyweight, barbell pressing, squats, 'good mornings', and twisting with a barbell not only do not enhance, but to a certain degree, will e\n70\t\n71\t### claim--traditional-circuit-weight-training-gives-only-modest-mixed-benefits\n72\tchapter: \"5.6\"\n73\tconfidence: supported\n74\t**What it is** — The authors' verdict on the \"average commercial circuit\": \"only a moderate compromise of the reputed cardiovascular and strength conditioning benefits can be achieved by traditional circuit training\" (PDF pp. 311-312 / printed pp. 300-301). Specifically: circuit weight training (CWT) \"using combinations of gymnasium apparatus with fixed bicycles or mini-trampolines cannot produce cardiovascular conditioning which rivals that of running, cycling and other traditional forms\"; \"because CWT generally relies on the use of light weights and high repetitions, increase in strength and power is also very limited\"; and \"Virtually the only significant benefits of the average commercial\n75\t\n76\t### framework--apre-autoregulating-progressive-resistance-exercise\n77\tchapter: \"5.2.2\"\n78\tconfidence: contested\n79\t**What it is** — APRE is the authors' expansion of Knight's (1979) DAPRE (\"Daily Adjustable Progressive Resistance Exercise\"), a DeLorme variant \"used successfully in physiotherapy\" that \"offers a combination of training and testing for the recovering patient.\" DAPRE: set 1, 10 reps at 50% of the anticipated 6RM; set 2, 6 reps at 75%; set 3, the anticipated 6RM to failure; the reps in set 3 set the load for set 4 by an adjustment table, and set 4's reps set the next workout's starting weight (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261, verified against the page image). The authors say DAPRE \"needs to be expanded somewhat\" to fit \"the principles of supercompensation and functional fitness,\" because \"daily t\n80\t\n81\t### framework--circuit-layout-variants-superset-retrophase-zigzag-detour\n82\tchapter: \"5.6\"\n83\tconfidence: untested\n84\t**What it is** — The authors propose \"a systems approach adapted from applied mathematics\" to circuits. \"The traditional approach requires one to proceed from starting station to terminal station for a certain number of sets, without backtracking, omitting stations or diverting to extra equipment\"; \"The more generalised approach includes the possibility of backtracking, omitting stations, altering the sequence of stations, diverting to other equipment, using different speeds of movement or time periods on each station, changing the exercise pattern at each station, and changing the types of muscle contraction at each station\" (PDF p. 320 / printed p. 309, checked against the page image). The\n85\t\n86\t### framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading\n87\tchapter: \"5.3.3\"\n88\tconfidence: contested\n89\t**What it is** — The authors set out the working conditions for the concentrated phases of the conjugate sequence system (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294, checked against the page image): 1. **Scale and duration.** \"Unidirectional emphasis on qualities such as strength or endurance may be used not only in individual workouts but also during each microcycle (Kudelin, 1980; Budokho et al, 1978; Mironenko, 1980). Research has shown that unidirectional training over periods as long as 4-12 weeks can be very effective, especially if the intensity is gradually increased for each form of loading in the given cycle.\" 2. **Diversity within one emphasis.** \"unidirectional loading is effective only if one \n90\t\n91\t### framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means\n92\tchapter: \"5.3.3\"\n93\tconfidence: contested\n94\t**What it is** — The authors name \"two main systems of organising long term training: the concurrent system and the conjugate sequence system\" (PDF p. 303 / printed p. 292). The concurrent system is \"the parallel training of several motor abilities, such as strength, speed and endurance, over the same period.\" The conjugate sequence system (\"sometimes translated from the Russian as the coupled successive system\") \"involves successively introducing into the training programme separate, specific means, each of which has a progressively stronger training effect, and coupling them sequentially to create favourable conditions for eliciting the cumulative effect of all the training loads.\" It usua\n95\t\n96\t### framework--continuous-interval-and-mixed-circuit-training-classification\n97\tchapter: \"5.6\"\n98\tconfidence: untested\n99\t**What it is** — Having argued that traditional circuits fail because they remove rest, the authors extend the concept: \"if rest intervals are re-introduced into the system, CT can be extended enormously to produce a far more versatile training modality.\" They call the new form Interval Circuit Training (ICT), \"familiar to all runners in the form of interval training and fartlek.\" A circuit is \"really an extended form of 'supersetting'\"; \"the term 'super circuit' should then be reserved for the type of ICT system which supersets a series of resistance exercises\" (PDF pp. 311, 313 / printed pp. 300, 302). The classification (PDF p. 313 / printed p. 302, checked against the page image): - **A.\n100\t\n101\t### framework--four-principles-of-special-strength-training-organisation\n102\tchapter: \"5.4\"\n103\tconfidence: supported\n104\t**What it is** — Section 5.4 opens with the premise that adaptation changes character with level. \"During the initial stages of training the body reacts to any stimulus with all of its survival mechanisms and motor abilities,\" there is \"a relatively high correlation between the various motor abilities,\" and \"The transfer of fitness is very distinct here.\" \"As sporting proficiency grows, so the adaptation response becomes more selective,\" the link between abilities weakens, and \"the direction of progress is determined entirely by the specific components of the training stimulus\" (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294, checked against the page image). On that basis the authors give \"a set of general pri\n105\t\n106\t### framework--plyometric-training-classification-impact-intensity-and-function\n107\tchapter: \"5.2.4\"\n108\tconfidence: supported\n109\t**What it is** — The text (Siff's classification; it uses his term \"powermetrics\", PDF p. 279 / printed p. 268) replaces the \"classically plyometric or not\" argument with a classification (PDF pp. 283-284 / printed pp. 272-273; Fig 5.8 verified against the page image): - **Impact versus non-impact.** In impact plyometrics \"the rebound is stimulated by contact with a surface or object\"; in non-impact plyometrics the recoil \"is mediated by explosive eccentric action of the muscles that produce a movement not terminated by contact with a surface.\" Depth jumps are impact; \"snap kicks or rapidly-retracted blows in boxing or the martial arts\" are non-impact, which \"may also be maximal or submaxima\n110\t\n111\t### framework--sequential-and-concurrent-variants-of-systematic-means\n112\tchapter: \"5.3\"\n113\tconfidence: supported\n114\t**What it is** — The authors state that \"The systematic use of means of special strength training over a given period produces a cumulative effect which is significantly greater than the separate non-sequential application of these same means,\" and that there are \"two possible variants for the systematic application of means: sequential and concurrent\" (PDF p. 298 / printed p. 287). 1. **Sequential means:** \"the strictly determined, successive introduction of different means, each with a higher training effect, into the annual or multi-annual cycle.\" Its basis is two observations: \"the training effect of any means diminishes as the body adapts to its influence\" and \"the training effect must \n115\t\n116\t### framework--specific-correspondence-of-the-training-effect-four-tasks\n117\tchapter: \"5.4.3\"\n118\tconfidence: supported\n119\t**What it is** — \"The principle of specific correspondence points to the need for correspondence between the general effect of the strength work and the specific character of the motor regime of the given sport,\" achieved by \"systematically organising means so that the resulting cumulative effect fully establishes the special strength fitness required at the current stage of the sports training process\" (PDF p. 307 / printed p. 296). The body \"selectively reacts to the dominant motor regime and movement pattern,\" which \"causes local, functional improvement in the fitness qualities of the kinematic chain's motor system (specialisation of systems) and develops qualities which are appropriate t\n120\t\n121\t### framework--three-methods-for-maximum-strength-repetitive-brief-maximal-isometric\n122\tchapter: \"5.2.1\"\n123\tconfidence: contested\n124\t**What it is** — Maximum strength in this chapter is \"the maximal force that a person can develop voluntarily,\" shown \"under isometric conditions or in a very slow movement against a maximal load.\" There are \"two basic methods\": \"the method of repetitive effort and the method of brief maximal tension\" (PDF p. 269 / printed p. 258), with isometrics discussed as a third option (PDF p. 271-272 / printed p. 260-261). The authors' summary (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261, verified against the page image): - **Repetitive effort**: lifting a weight that is raised as strength grows; \"appropriate in the initial stages of strength development and when strength and its speed of production are not important\n125\t\n126\t### framework--two-groups-of-speed-strength-movements-and-their-tension-types\n127\tchapter: \"5.2.3\"\n128\tconfidence: supported\n129\t**What it is** — The authors call speed-strength \"highly generalised and context-dependent\" and divide speed-strength movements \"roughly into two fundamental groups\": \"Movements in which speed of movement plays a fundamental role in overcoming a relatively small resistance\" and \"Movements in which effort is rapidly developed to overcome large resistance.\" \"Maximum strength is generally unimportant for the first type of movement, but it plays a decisive role in the second.\" The first group includes movements with \"a rapid reaction to external stimuli with brief contractions or with frequent repetitive contractions.\" The second is split by tension type (3.1): \"explosive-isometric tension (asso\n130\t\n131\t### heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next\n132\tchapter: \"5.3.3\"\n133\tconfidence: contested\n134\t**What it is** — The authors warn: \"The system is not without its dangers, since concentrated loading is very stressful and, besides increasing the potential for overtraining, it can decrease motor qualities such as speed-strength. After a phase of emphasis on heavy strength training, there may be an increase in muscle stiffness and a loss of explosive strength, sporting skill or speed.\" Their rule, italicised in the original: \"a diminished loading and restorative period has to be observed after a given concentrated loading phase to allow the body to supercompensate to the desired level of preparedness.\" The timing: \"The gains elicited, for instance, by 4-8 week concentrated blocks of streng\n135\t\n136\t### heuristic--analytical-synthetic-and-multifaceted-preparation-suit-only-early-training\n137\tchapter: \"5.1\"\n138\tconfidence: supported\n139\t**What it is** — The authors describe the first attempts to train the combined qualities as the \"analytical-synthetic method\": \"the qualities should be developed separately with the appropriate means and then they should be integrated into the special exercises\" (analysis breaks a process into components; synthesis combines them into a whole \"different from each of its components\"). Examples: sprints for gymnasts and weightlifters, barbell work for track athletes, and cyclic sports for general endurance (Verkhoshansky 1977). They grant these recommendations are hard to deny, but say they \"are appropriate only for the initial stages of training and it would be a serious error for highly-quali\n140\t\n141\t### heuristic--apre-skill-learning-6rm-pyramid-test-and-rm-muscle-testing\n142\tchapter: \"5.2.2\"\n143\tconfidence: untested\n144\t**What it is** — The authors' protocol around APRE (PDF p. 273 and 275 / printed p. 262 and 264, verified against the page images). **Pre-testing**: \"a skills/learning phase of about two weeks is advisable\": a warm-up without weights, then \"2-3 sets comprising 8-10 repetitions with approximately 20% of bodymass for males and 10% of bodymass for females,\" heavier for large muscle groups (\"quadriceps, pectorals and erector spinae\") and lighter for small ones; in week two \"these percentages might easily be doubled\"; on the last day, test the 6RM. **Testing**: a pyramid \"beginning with 10 repetitions with approximately 40% of bodymass for males and 20% for females,\" then more weight for 8 reps, \n145\t\n146\t### heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size\n147\tchapter: \"5.2.4\"\n148\tconfidence: contested\n149\t**What it is** — The authors' method-selection rules (PDF p. 289 / printed p. 278, verified against the page image unless marked): - \"In those cases where explosive force is associated with the overcoming of large resistance, preference should be given to the brief maximal tension method, as used commonly in Olympic weightlifting. In situations where the resistance is small and the working effect depends upon the reactive ability of the muscles, plyometric methods should be given preference.\" - Staging: \"Although it should form an integral part of the training programme, plyometrics should not be combined in the beginning stages with resistance exercises such as the brief maximal tension met\n150\t\n151\t### heuristic--choose-cross-training-by-fitness-qualities-and-phase-not-sport-label\n152\tchapter: \"5.5\"\n153\tconfidence: supported\n154\t**What it is** — The authors define Western \"cross training\" as \"using relevant secondary sports to augment the training effect produced in an athlete's primary sport,\" and tie it to the two ways of combining training from 5.3: supplementary activities can be prescribed \"concurrently with the athlete's main sport on the same day or during the same training phase or in a definite sequence where one supplementary activity gives way to another and finally to the main sport over a given period\" (PDF p. 309 / printed p. 298). In Russia and Eastern Europe, they write, the choice \"does not simply focus primarily on the type of sport used, but rather on the relationship between the motor and fitness\n155\t\n156\t### heuristic--depth-jump-height-set-by-transition-speed-and-heel-contact\n157\tchapter: \"5.2.4\"\n158\tconfidence: supported\n159\t**What it is** — The authors' rules for choosing drop height in training (all on pages verified against the page image): - \"The optimal depth of the jump is determined by the athlete's strength fitness, while the athlete should ensure that significant dynamic force is developed without slowing down the transition from eccentric to concentric work by the muscles involved\" (PDF p. 285 / printed p. 274). - \"The optimal height of a depth jump does not result in a landing where the heel is forced to the ground by momentum\" (PDF p. 287 / printed p. 276, item 8). - \"One should aim to increase the speed and acceleration of movement before increasing the drop height or take-off height\" (PDF p. 285 / \n160\t\n161\t### heuristic--depth-jump-weekly-frequency-and-seasonal-placement\n162\tchapter: \"5.2.4\"\n163\tconfidence: contested\n164\t**What it is** — From the authors' depth-jump recommendations (PDF pp. 286-287 / printed pp. 275-276, verified against the page images): - **Weekly frequency (item 3):** depth jumps \"in the volume indicated should be done once or twice a week in training sessions devoted to special strength training\", which can also include \"localised exercises for other muscle groups and general developmental exercises executed with a small volume.\" But \"Well-conditioned athletes can execute depth-jumps 3 times per week for 2 sets of 10 jumps at the completion of technical training in the particular sport.\" - **Spacing from technique (item 4):** \"Depth-jumps strongly excite the nervous system. Therefore, th\n165\t\n166\t### heuristic--dynamic-correspondence-means-matching-motor-characteristics-not-copying-the-sport-movement\n167\tchapter: \"5.1\"\n168\tconfidence: supported\n169\t**What it is** — The authors warn of \"an excessive passion for making the strength training means correspond structurally to the special exercises.\" The misinterpretation of dynamic correspondence \"lies in literally copying the special exercise in training movements, instead of selecting a specific arsenal of means corresponding to the most important motor characteristics of the given sport and controlling their growth by creating conditions under which they will develop steadily.\" In their words, \"it is inappropriate to try to simulate sports specific movements with supplementary training activities,\" because this \"not only does ... not accurately develop the necessary fitness and motor abi\n170\t\n171\t### heuristic--early-specialisation-by-motor-regime-not-by-sport\n172\tchapter: \"5.4.1\"\n173\tconfidence: untested\n174\t**What it is** — The authors apply the convergence principle to youth: \"particular consideration was given to the advisability of early specialisation, not in the specific type of sport, but in the specific motor regime involved\" (Verkhoshansky 1963, 1972 cited). \"orientation of the child to the sport and physical training with the sports means does not begin with specialised exercise to achieve competitive performance standards (which can easily lead to forced training). Instead, it begins with systematic management of the functional perfecting of the body\" (PDF p. 306 / printed p. 295, checked against the page image). The model has two stages: 1. A child who shows \"a predisposition to a pa\n175\t\n176\t### heuristic--equip-circuits-with-free-weights-and-pulleys-over-multistation-machines\n177\tchapter: \"5.6\"\n178\tconfidence: supported\n179\t**What it is** — For circuits, the authors rank equipment by cost and function. Where cost matters, \"specialised variable and non-variable multi-purpose machines have to be regarded as the lowest priority,\" and \"a few inexpensive 'hi-lo' pulley machines would be a more than adequate substitute. In fact, the latter offer a level of three-dimensional variety that other machines cannot emulate.\" \"A well chosen collection of free weights, benches, racks and pulley machines, wedded with a skipping surface, padded aerobics area, bouncing boards and a few basic fixed cycles, offers virtually everything that the most impressive multi-station machines offer at a fraction of the price.\" \"Free weights \n180\t\n181\t### heuristic--isometric-apre-six-second-maximum-protocol\n182\tchapter: \"5.2.2\"\n183\tconfidence: supported\n184\t**What it is** — APRE \"may also be applied with an isometric training regime and may be especially valuable during the earliest stages of muscular rehabilitation, when dynamic activities may be inadvisable.\" The authors base the dose on Ivanov's data: \"the optimal combination of these variables is 6 repetitions of 100% of one's 1RM held for 6-8 seconds per repetition (Figs 5.2, 5.3)\" (PDF p. 274 / printed p. 263). The protocol: hold a weight isometrically \"for a 6SM (6 second maximum) at a specified joint angle (beginning at or near full extension in most cases), with a 5-10 second rest between repetitions.\" Set 1 at 50% of the 6SM weight, set 2 at 75%, set 3 \"until the weight cannot be held\n185\t\n186\t### heuristic--plyometric-training-coupling-under-0-15-s-feedforward-and-force-time-pattern\n187\tchapter: \"5.2.4\"\n188\tconfidence: supported\n189\t**What it is** — The authors give three tests for whether a drill is classical \"training plyometrics\" rather than ordinary jumping: 1. **Coupling time.** The method \"is characterised by impulsive action of minimal duration between the end of the eccentric braking phase and initiation of the concentric acceleration phase\". \"If the transition phase (or, coupling phase) is prolonged by more than about 0.15 second, the action may be considered to constitute ordinary jumping and not classical training plyometrics.\" The coaching image: imagine the contact surface \"is red hot, so that any prolonged contact would be dangerous.\" (PDF p. 279 / printed p. 268, verified against the page image.) 2. **Fee\n190\t\n191\t### heuristic--rate-plyometric-intensity-by-contact-momentum-from-drop-height\n192\tchapter: \"5.2.4\"\n193\tconfidence: supported\n194\t**What it is** — The text (Siff, using his term \"powermetrics\", PDF p. 279 / printed p. 268) proposes \"a *powermetrics intensity scale* similar to that used in weightlifting, where exercises are ranked in terms of percentages of one's 1RM.\" Because \"the concept of 1RMs is meaningless in plyometric training\", one must use estimates of muscle tension or of \"the impulse ... produced during the contact phase\". \"Impulse is equivalent to the change of momentum (m.ΔV) during the amortisation phase, so that its magnitude for a falling body depends on the height from which the body drops. Thus, the momentum just before contact (estimated from height of the drop jump or distance fallen by the object) \n195\t\n196\t### heuristic--replace-means-as-their-training-effect-fades\n197\tchapter: \"5.4.4\"\n198\tconfidence: supported\n199\t**What it is** — \"Preservation of the training effect means that the load should always produce a training effect by the systematic, timely introduction of more effective training means based on logical continuity,\" to avoid \"the states of stagnation and deterioration described in Chapter 1 (see Fig 1.32).\" In practice, \"as the training effect of some means decreases, so more effective, newer means are introduced which, in turn, are replaced by even more effective means.\" This needs \"experimental assessment of the training effect and categorisation of the specific complex of means\" (PDF p. 308 / printed p. 297). Fig 5.18 (verified against the page image) gives the authors' example for \"devel\n200\t\n201\t### heuristic--speed-strength-loading-light-loads-maximal-acceleration-and-5-to-1-mix\n202\tchapter: \"5.2.3\"\n203\tconfidence: supported\n204\t**What it is** — \"The development of speed-strength is more effective the more speed loading there is in training and the shorter the time spent using slow movements (Yakovlev et al, 1960).\" \"Exercise with small loads (with approximately 20% of 1RM) is the principal method of developing speed-strength\" (Korobkov 1953; Vasiliev 1954; Gerasimov, Yakhontov 1954; Zimkin 1956; Agdgomelashvili 1964; Butenko 1967) (PDF p. 275-276 / printed p. 264-265, verified against the page images). With it, \"speed of movement increases with and without loads, and experiments have shown that the general increase in speed can reach 146% of the initial level.\" \"Movements should be executed with maximal effort and \n205\t\n206\t### heuristic--strength-endurance-load-tempo-and-the-75-80-percent-ceiling\n207\tchapter: \"5.2.5\"\n208\tconfidence: contested\n209\t**What it is** — Section 5.2.5 (PDF pp. 296-298 / printed pp. 285-287; pp. 296-298 verified against the page images): - **Eight determinants.** Training for strength-endurance \"is determined predominantly by: 1. The magnitude of the load 2. The tempo of movement 3. The duration of each movement and each phase of the movements 4. The rest interval between training sessions 5. The length of the training period 6. The initial levels of strength-endurance of the athlete 7. The kinesiological structure of the movement 8. The relative involvement of static and dynamic strength-endurance\" (PDF p. 296 / printed p. 285). - **Basic prescription.** \"Repetition work with weights 25-50% of 1RM at a moder\n210\t\n211\t### heuristic--strength-endurance-under-demanding-sport-like-conditions\n212\tchapter: \"5.2.5\"\n213\tconfidence: supported\n214\t**What it is** — \"For developing strength-endurance it is always appropriate to strive to execute the work under demanding conditions, though with movements whose coordination and structure in space and time are close to those of the special exercise. For instance, a runner may run with a small parachute resisting him or pull a loaded sled in the preparatory phases (Popov, 1966). Similarly, gymnasts may use a heavy belt and vest (Zhilei, 1964) and rowers a special water-resistance device attached to the hull of the boat (Chuprun, 1966). In the case of the rowers, strength-endurance as measured by the number of pull-ups, the number of arm flexions in the prone position and the number of barbe\n215\t\n216\t### principle--converging-partial-effects-of-strength-training-means\n217\tchapter: \"5.4.1\"\n218\tconfidence: supported\n219\t**What it is** — \"The principle of converging the partial effects of strength training means is fundamental to the long-term organisation of strength training. It is achieved by ... the gradual convergence of their cumulative training effect to those important specific characteristics which are essential to the functioning of the body in a particular sport\" (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294). Early in the sports training process (STP), \"the tasks which determine the diversity of the training means are far apart,\" linked \"only by the long-term plan.\" Specialisation then \"is firstly according to ability (but not to the physiological system) and is not related to the motor pattern (see 2.2.3).\" \"As \n220\t\n221\t### principle--sequentially-introduce-means-of-greater-training-effect\n222\tchapter: \"5.3.2\"\n223\tconfidence: supported\n224\t**What it is** — In 5.3.2, \"A Sequential System of Training Means,\" the authors argue that the development of a specific motor ability \"occurs in a specific pattern,\" so \"it is necessary to sequentially improve the functioning of those systems and physiological mechanisms which are crucial for developing a specific motor ability\" (PDF p. 302 / printed p. 291). \"Progressive functional changes in some systems act as prerequisites for improving the performance of other systems.\" The rule: \"the means used should elicit a definite training effect, but its magnitude should correspond to the body's current level of specific fitness and increase in conjunction with the latter.\" Hence \"the necessity \n225\t\n226\t### principle--speed-strength-strength-endurance-and-speed-endurance-are-independent-qualities\n227\tchapter: \"5.1\"\n228\tconfidence: supported\n229\t**What it is** — The authors state that \"speed-strength, strength-endurance and speed-endurance are not simply derivatives of strength, speed and endurance, but are totally independent qualities.\" Their evidence is that \"an increase in absolute strength does not necessarily enhance any of these three qualities.\" So the combined qualities \"should be placed alongside the latter in a group, each with its own particular means and methods of development (Verkhoshansky, 1963)\" (PDF p. 268 / printed p. 257). The same paragraph frames force and velocity as qualities that, \"at a high level of development are inversely proportional to one another (see Ch 4),\" and says excessive development of both \"is\n230\t\n231\t"}]},"uuid":"519a83db-124d-468e-9b41-ae10824caae1","timestamp":"2026-10-03T17:06:19.758Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/projects/-Users-kristinarash-DEV/9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c/tool-results/biq0w8b0l.txt","content":"### case-study--alternate-leg-bounding-with-thigh-cuffs-for-sprinters\nchapter: \"5.2.3\"\nconfidence: untested\n**What it is** — \"In certain cases, power can be increased without using additional loads. For example, with jumps from one leg to the other, the push-off displays values of power which are greater than in running. Therefore, these jumps are excellent for the special strength training of sprinters.\" They must be done \"by not accentuating the push-off of the body (which does not happen in sprinting), but by active gathering-in of the planted leg (i.e. via hip flexion).\" \"These jumps should be executed repetitively over distances of 50-100 meters as fast as possible. The addition of small cuffs weighing from 100-150 grams on each thigh can makes such exercises more effective in experienced ath\n\n### case-study--arm-throw-height-after-barbell-press-tonic-work\nchapter: \"5.2.3\"\nconfidence: untested\n**What it is** — \"The progressive development of speed-strength is the result of combining means for exploiting the after-effect phenomenon of preceding work to increase the effectiveness of subsequent work.\" In an \"exercise modelling\" experiment, \"the working effect of an explosive movement, as measured by the height to which a load was thrown (Fig 5.4), increases by an average of 38-40% after execution of barbell presses for 3 sets of 3 repetitions with 80% of the 1RM (with a rest interval of 10 minutes between the pressing and the throwing),\" with shorter movement time and greater working path, average speed, driving force and power (PDF p. 276 / printed p. 265). Table 5.3, \"Arm movement \n\n### case-study--deniskin-depth-jumps-after-resistance-and-rest-as-a-training-means\nchapter: \"5.3\"\nconfidence: supported\n**What it is** — \"qualified athletes modelled different variants of weightlifting pre-competition training under laboratory conditions (Deniskin, 1976).\" The test movement was \"A leg extension movement involving the lifting of a 1RM ... starting with an initial knee angle of 90°.\" Stage 1 was \"intensive work with resistance, the purpose of which was to increase explosive strength.\" \"Only depth-jumps were used during the second and third stages.\" Two depth-jump stages were used because \"a preliminary investigation ... showed that the rise in functional state achieved after one stage is unstable.\" \"The fourth stage served as a control and served only to test performance.\" \"The subjects were gi\n\n### case-study--novice-strength-and-speed-strength-sequencing-experiments\nchapter: \"5.3\"\nconfidence: contested\n**What it is** — The authors report a set of experiments on novices in speed-strength sports (researchers named as \"Purvin, Khodykin, Deniskin, Tatian and Chernousov\", names checked against the page image; no dates, sample sizes or statistics are given) (PDF p. 299 / printed p. 288): 1. **Order across the preparatory period.** \"the sequence of means in the preparatory period (beginning with strength, then speed-strength means, or vice-versa) has no real significance for novices. Consequently, the same effect can be obtained by using these means in any order.\" 2. **Mixed sessions.** \"A relatively larger effect than produced by the above-mentioned variants (with the same volume of training loa\n\n### claim--depth-jump-training-beat-higher-volume-traditional-training-in-jumpers\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\nconfidence: supported\n**What it is** — \"The plyometric method of developing explosive strength and reactive ability solves to a significant degree the problem of economising training, since it ensures that a high level of special conditioning is attained in the minimum time. Thus, a comparative experiment revealed that a group of track-and-field jumpers executing primarily depth-jumps (all of them did 475 jumps) over a 12 week period in the preparation phase showed greater improvement in reactive ability than the group which trained with traditional methods and executed 1472 general push-offs (squats, jumping and hopping with a barbell weighing 90-95%, 70-80% and 30-40% of 1RM) and lifted a total volume of 93 ton\n\n### claim--excess-heavy-strength-volume-impairs-speed-strength-and-technique\nchapter: \"5.3.1\"\nconfidence: supported\n**What it is** — The authors grant that general heavy resistance exercises \"enable the athlete to reach the necessary level of absolute strength, to provide general physical conditioning and to train the neuromuscular system non-specifically,\" but \"it is undesirable to use them extensively during specialised training phases, since they can diminish speed and explosiveness. High volume strength loads used after a phase of specialised jump training can substantially reduce the existing level of explosive strength (by as much as 30%)\" (PDF p. 302 / printed p. 291; all figures in this entry checked against the page image). Supporting reports, all cited without dates except Deniskin: Kotz, \"prolo\n\n### claim--heavy-squats-and-light-fast-lifts-miss-the-eccentric-concentric-switch\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\nconfidence: supported\n**What it is** — The authors open 5.2.4 by analysing the traditional speed-strength means. In heavy barbell squats \"the muscles work very slowly (quasi-isometrically) with a relatively constant tension produced by the load. Consequently, the muscles primarily develop isometric strength\". Raising the barbell weight, \"frequently considered a basic indicator of special strength fitness, places a large and generally unnecessary load on the spine.\" For speed-strength, athletes use smaller loads; their example is \"squat thrusts with a 60kg barbell on the shoulders\", which \"produces a large dynamic maximum force\". So \"exercises with heavy weights tend to increase the strength potential of the muscl\n\n### claim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-keep-technique-and-size-per-athlete\nchapter: \"5.2.3\"\nconfidence: supported\n**What it is** — For acyclic speed-strength the authors say \"plyometric or throwing exercises should be selected on the basis of their relevance to the sports activity,\" and give a run of implement findings (PDF p. 276-277 / printed p. 265-266, verified against the page images). Water polo throw: a 2 kg medicine ball beat a 4 kg ball, throwing distance up \"13.6% and 8.9%, respectively,\" and \"throwing a 4 kg ball is detrimental to technique (Rogener, 1961).\" Baseball: a light \"57 grams\" and heavy \"184 gm\" ball both improved throwing (regulation \"about 148 gm\"), but \"throwing the light ball improves precision for throwing the heavier ball, but the reverse effect does not occur (Egstrom et al, \n\n### claim--ivanov-1966-contraction-mode-and-which-strength-test-improves\nchapter: \"5.2.1\"\nconfidence: supported\n**What it is** — In \"A 3-month experiment comparing the effectiveness of concentric, eccentric and isometric work,\" \"the largest increase in a 1RM squat occurred with eccentric work (average 15 kg), and the smallest with isometric tension (9.2 kg).\" \"Not unexpectedly, the largest increase in isometric strength was obtained with isometric tension (30.2 kg), and the smallest increase with concentric work (14.6 kg).\" \"Vertical jump increased only with the concentric training regime (3.7 cm)\"; it \"decreased by 1.6 cm for the eccentric regime and by 5.4 cm for isometric tension (Ivanov, 1966)\" (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261, numbers verified against the page image; the text layer misreads 30.2 as \"\n\n### claim--moderate-and-mixed-movement-tempo-for-repetition-strength-gains\nchapter: \"5.2.1\"\nconfidence: supported\n**What it is** — The authors report that \"The movement tempo of resistance exercises also has great significance for the development of muscular strength (Vorobyev, 1978).\" With a moderate tempo \"strength continues to increase over a period of 30 training sessions, whereas it increases only over a period of 15 sessions with other tempos,\" and \"the most effective variant is a combination of different movement tempos\": over 10 weeks the gain \"was 22.6 ± 0.6 kg; and 16.3 ± 0.5 kg for the execution of movements at a moderate tempo for both concentric and eccentric actions (Lelikov, 1975)\" (PDF p. 271 / printed p. 260, verified against the page image). In Vasiliev (1954), after 40 sessions of equ\n\n### claim--newton-ii-and-impulse-momentum-as-the-math-behind-the-force-time-curve\nchapter: \"5.1\"\nconfidence: proven\n**What it is** — The authors open Chapter 5 by saying that if \"the Force-Time curve is to be regarded as the graphical starting point for sport biomechanics, then Newton's 2nd Law of Motion must logically serve as the corresponding mathematical starting point\" (PDF p. 266 / printed p. 255). They state Newton II as \"Force is proportional to the rate of change of momentum,\" write it as equation (5.1), F = d(m.v)/dt = m.dv/dt + v.dm/dt, and reduce it to F = m.a \"if the mass of the system is not changing with time,\" which they say holds in sport (PDF p. 266-267 / printed p. 255-256; equations verified against the page image). They then separate force \"generated ballistically or impulsively\" from\n\n### claim--plyometric-skill-is-cognitive-and-state-dependent\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\nconfidence: untested\n**What it is** — Under \"Plyometrics and the Brain\" (PDF pp. 294-295 / printed pp. 283-284, text layer): - \"plyometric action is not simply a phenomenon that can be adequately explained on the basis of stored elastic energy and the stretch shortening cycle.\" Although the action itself is fast and spinal, \"the preparatory period prior to the plyometric action can involve higher order mental processing of exceptional intricacy.\" - **Cognitive versus non-cognitive plyometrics:** a plyometric action \"under conditions in which the athlete anticipates a particular course of action (*cognitive plyometrics*)\" versus \"a purely reflexive response under unexpected circumstances (*non-cognitive plyometri\n\n### claim--prestretch-benefit-decay-time-differs-by-task\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\nconfidence: contested\n**What it is** — After stating the about 0.15 s coupling guide, the authors say it \"requires some qualification\" and list conflicting results (PDF pp. 281-282 / printed pp. 270-271; Figs 5.6 and 5.7 verified against the page image): - Wilson et al (1990), bench press: \"the benefits of prior stretch may endure for as long as 4 seconds, at which stage it is suggested that all stored elastic energy is lost (see Fig 5.6).\" - Chapman and Caldwell (1985), forearm movement: benefits \"dissipated within 0.25 second, a figure which agrees with Siff's analysis of explosive rebound elbow flexion without additional loading (Fig 5.7).\" - Wilson et al (1991), rebound action of the chest and arms: \"no benef\n\n### claim--strength-endurance-hypertrophy-and-cyclic-endurance\nchapter: \"5.2.5\"\nconfidence: contested\n**What it is** — From 5.2.5 (PDF pp. 297-298 / printed pp. 286-287, verified against the page images): - \"an increase in strength-endurance contributes to improving endurance in distance runners, skiers and skaters (Popov, 1968; Nabatnikova, 1972; Mikhailov & Panov, 1975). However ... the magnitude of the strength load used is different if, on the one hand, the key quality required is general endurance and, on the other hand, it is strength-endurance.\" - \"certain popular strength exercises such as running with a sack of sand weighing 25% of the subject's bodyweight, barbell pressing, squats, 'good mornings', and twisting with a barbell not only do not enhance, but to a certain degree, will e\n\n### claim--traditional-circuit-weight-training-gives-only-modest-mixed-benefits\nchapter: \"5.6\"\nconfidence: supported\n**What it is** — The authors' verdict on the \"average commercial circuit\": \"only a moderate compromise of the reputed cardiovascular and strength conditioning benefits can be achieved by traditional circuit training\" (PDF pp. 311-312 / printed pp. 300-301). Specifically: circuit weight training (CWT) \"using combinations of gymnasium apparatus with fixed bicycles or mini-trampolines cannot produce cardiovascular conditioning which rivals that of running, cycling and other traditional forms\"; \"because CWT generally relies on the use of light weights and high repetitions, increase in strength and power is also very limited\"; and \"Virtually the only significant benefits of the average commercial\n\n### framework--apre-autoregulating-progressive-resistance-exercise\nchapter: \"5.2.2\"\nconfidence: contested\n**What it is** — APRE is the authors' expansion of Knight's (1979) DAPRE (\"Daily Adjustable Progressive Resistance Exercise\"), a DeLorme variant \"used successfully in physiotherapy\" that \"offers a combination of training and testing for the recovering patient.\" DAPRE: set 1, 10 reps at 50% of the anticipated 6RM; set 2, 6 reps at 75%; set 3, the anticipated 6RM to failure; the reps in set 3 set the load for set 4 by an adjustment table, and set 4's reps set the next workout's starting weight (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261, verified against the page image). The authors say DAPRE \"needs to be expanded somewhat\" to fit \"the principles of supercompensation and functional fitness,\" because \"daily t\n\n### framework--circuit-layout-variants-superset-retrophase-zigzag-detour\nchapter: \"5.6\"\nconfidence: untested\n**What it is** — The authors propose \"a systems approach adapted from applied mathematics\" to circuits. \"The traditional approach requires one to proceed from starting station to terminal station for a certain number of sets, without backtracking, omitting stations or diverting to extra equipment\"; \"The more generalised approach includes the possibility of backtracking, omitting stations, altering the sequence of stations, diverting to other equipment, using different speeds of movement or time periods on each station, changing the exercise pattern at each station, and changing the types of muscle contraction at each station\" (PDF p. 320 / printed p. 309, checked against the page image). The\n\n### framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading\nchapter: \"5.3.3\"\nconfidence: contested\n**What it is** — The authors set out the working conditions for the concentrated phases of the conjugate sequence system (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294, checked against the page image): 1. **Scale and duration.** \"Unidirectional emphasis on qualities such as strength or endurance may be used not only in individual workouts but also during each microcycle (Kudelin, 1980; Budokho et al, 1978; Mironenko, 1980). Research has shown that unidirectional training over periods as long as 4-12 weeks can be very effective, especially if the intensity is gradually increased for each form of loading in the given cycle.\" 2. **Diversity within one emphasis.** \"unidirectional loading is effective only if one \n\n### framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means\nchapter: \"5.3.3\"\nconfidence: contested\n**What it is** — The authors name \"two main systems of organising long term training: the concurrent system and the conjugate sequence system\" (PDF p. 303 / printed p. 292). The concurrent system is \"the parallel training of several motor abilities, such as strength, speed and endurance, over the same period.\" The conjugate sequence system (\"sometimes translated from the Russian as the coupled successive system\") \"involves successively introducing into the training programme separate, specific means, each of which has a progressively stronger training effect, and coupling them sequentially to create favourable conditions for eliciting the cumulative effect of all the training loads.\" It usua\n\n### framework--continuous-interval-and-mixed-circuit-training-classification\nchapter: \"5.6\"\nconfidence: untested\n**What it is** — Having argued that traditional circuits fail because they remove rest, the authors extend the concept: \"if rest intervals are re-introduced into the system, CT can be extended enormously to produce a far more versatile training modality.\" They call the new form Interval Circuit Training (ICT), \"familiar to all runners in the form of interval training and fartlek.\" A circuit is \"really an extended form of 'supersetting'\"; \"the term 'super circuit' should then be reserved for the type of ICT system which supersets a series of resistance exercises\" (PDF pp. 311, 313 / printed pp. 300, 302). The classification (PDF p. 313 / printed p. 302, checked against the page image): - **A.\n\n### framework--four-principles-of-special-strength-training-organisation\nchapter: \"5.4\"\nconfidence: supported\n**What it is** — Section 5.4 opens with the premise that adaptation changes character with level. \"During the initial stages of training the body reacts to any stimulus with all of its survival mechanisms and motor abilities,\" there is \"a relatively high correlation between the various motor abilities,\" and \"The transfer of fitness is very distinct here.\" \"As sporting proficiency grows, so the adaptation response becomes more selective,\" the link between abilities weakens, and \"the direction of progress is determined entirely by the specific components of the training stimulus\" (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294, checked against the page image). On that basis the authors give \"a set of general pri\n\n### framework--plyometric-training-classification-impact-intensity-and-function\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\nconfidence: supported\n**What it is** — The text (Siff's classification; it uses his term \"powermetrics\", PDF p. 279 / printed p. 268) replaces the \"classically plyometric or not\" argument with a classification (PDF pp. 283-284 / printed pp. 272-273; Fig 5.8 verified against the page image): - **Impact versus non-impact.** In impact plyometrics \"the rebound is stimulated by contact with a surface or object\"; in non-impact plyometrics the recoil \"is mediated by explosive eccentric action of the muscles that produce a movement not terminated by contact with a surface.\" Depth jumps are impact; \"snap kicks or rapidly-retracted blows in boxing or the martial arts\" are non-impact, which \"may also be maximal or submaxima\n\n### framework--sequential-and-concurrent-variants-of-systematic-means\nchapter: \"5.3\"\nconfidence: supported\n**What it is** — The authors state that \"The systematic use of means of special strength training over a given period produces a cumulative effect which is significantly greater than the separate non-sequential application of these same means,\" and that there are \"two possible variants for the systematic application of means: sequential and concurrent\" (PDF p. 298 / printed p. 287). 1. **Sequential means:** \"the strictly determined, successive introduction of different means, each with a higher training effect, into the annual or multi-annual cycle.\" Its basis is two observations: \"the training effect of any means diminishes as the body adapts to its influence\" and \"the training effect must \n\n### framework--specific-correspondence-of-the-training-effect-four-tasks\nchapter: \"5.4.3\"\nconfidence: supported\n**What it is** — \"The principle of specific correspondence points to the need for correspondence between the general effect of the strength work and the specific character of the motor regime of the given sport,\" achieved by \"systematically organising means so that the resulting cumulative effect fully establishes the special strength fitness required at the current stage of the sports training process\" (PDF p. 307 / printed p. 296). The body \"selectively reacts to the dominant motor regime and movement pattern,\" which \"causes local, functional improvement in the fitness qualities of the kinematic chain's motor system (specialisation of systems) and develops qualities which are appropriate t\n\n### framework--three-methods-for-maximum-strength-repetitive-brief-maximal-isometric\nchapter: \"5.2.1\"\nconfidence: contested\n**What it is** — Maximum strength in this chapter is \"the maximal force that a person can develop voluntarily,\" shown \"under isometric conditions or in a very slow movement against a maximal load.\" There are \"two basic methods\": \"the method of repetitive effort and the method of brief maximal tension\" (PDF p. 269 / printed p. 258), with isometrics discussed as a third option (PDF p. 271-272 / printed p. 260-261). The authors' summary (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261, verified against the page image): - **Repetitive effort**: lifting a weight that is raised as strength grows; \"appropriate in the initial stages of strength development and when strength and its speed of production are not important\n\n### framework--two-groups-of-speed-strength-movements-and-their-tension-types\nchapter: \"5.2.3\"\nconfidence: supported\n**What it is** — The authors call speed-strength \"highly generalised and context-dependent\" and divide speed-strength movements \"roughly into two fundamental groups\": \"Movements in which speed of movement plays a fundamental role in overcoming a relatively small resistance\" and \"Movements in which effort is rapidly developed to overcome large resistance.\" \"Maximum strength is generally unimportant for the first type of movement, but it plays a decisive role in the second.\" The first group includes movements with \"a rapid reaction to external stimuli with brief contractions or with frequent repetitive contractions.\" The second is split by tension type (3.1): \"explosive-isometric tension (asso\n\n### heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next\nchapter: \"5.3.3\"\nconfidence: contested\n**What it is** — The authors warn: \"The system is not without its dangers, since concentrated loading is very stressful and, besides increasing the potential for overtraining, it can decrease motor qualities such as speed-strength. After a phase of emphasis on heavy strength training, there may be an increase in muscle stiffness and a loss of explosive strength, sporting skill or speed.\" Their rule, italicised in the original: \"a diminished loading and restorative period has to be observed after a given concentrated loading phase to allow the body to supercompensate to the desired level of preparedness.\" The timing: \"The gains elicited, for instance, by 4-8 week concentrated blocks of streng\n\n### heuristic--analytical-synthetic-and-multifaceted-preparation-suit-only-early-training\nchapter: \"5.1\"\nconfidence: supported\n**What it is** — The authors describe the first attempts to train the combined qualities as the \"analytical-synthetic method\": \"the qualities should be developed separately with the appropriate means and then they should be integrated into the special exercises\" (analysis breaks a process into components; synthesis combines them into a whole \"different from each of its components\"). Examples: sprints for gymnasts and weightlifters, barbell work for track athletes, and cyclic sports for general endurance (Verkhoshansky 1977). They grant these recommendations are hard to deny, but say they \"are appropriate only for the initial stages of training and it would be a serious error for highly-quali\n\n### heuristic--apre-skill-learning-6rm-pyramid-test-and-rm-muscle-testing\nchapter: \"5.2.2\"\nconfidence: untested\n**What it is** — The authors' protocol around APRE (PDF p. 273 and 275 / printed p. 262 and 264, verified against the page images). **Pre-testing**: \"a skills/learning phase of about two weeks is advisable\": a warm-up without weights, then \"2-3 sets comprising 8-10 repetitions with approximately 20% of bodymass for males and 10% of bodymass for females,\" heavier for large muscle groups (\"quadriceps, pectorals and erector spinae\") and lighter for small ones; in week two \"these percentages might easily be doubled\"; on the last day, test the 6RM. **Testing**: a pyramid \"beginning with 10 repetitions with approximately 40% of bodymass for males and 20% for females,\" then more weight for 8 reps, \n\n### heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\nconfidence: contested\n**What it is** — The authors' method-selection rules (PDF p. 289 / printed p. 278, verified against the page image unless marked): - \"In those cases where explosive force is associated with the overcoming of large resistance, preference should be given to the brief maximal tension method, as used commonly in Olympic weightlifting. In situations where the resistance is small and the working effect depends upon the reactive ability of the muscles, plyometric methods should be given preference.\" - Staging: \"Although it should form an integral part of the training programme, plyometrics should not be combined in the beginning stages with resistance exercises such as the brief maximal tension met\n\n### heuristic--choose-cross-training-by-fitness-qualities-and-phase-not-sport-label\nchapter: \"5.5\"\nconfidence: supported\n**What it is** — The authors define Western \"cross training\" as \"using relevant secondary sports to augment the training effect produced in an athlete's primary sport,\" and tie it to the two ways of combining training from 5.3: supplementary activities can be prescribed \"concurrently with the athlete's main sport on the same day or during the same training phase or in a definite sequence where one supplementary activity gives way to another and finally to the main sport over a given period\" (PDF p. 309 / printed p. 298). In Russia and Eastern Europe, they write, the choice \"does not simply focus primarily on the type of sport used, but rather on the relationship between the motor and fitness\n\n### heuristic--depth-jump-height-set-by-transition-speed-and-heel-contact\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\nconfidence: supported\n**What it is** — The authors' rules for choosing drop height in training (all on pages verified against the page image): - \"The optimal depth of the jump is determined by the athlete's strength fitness, while the athlete should ensure that significant dynamic force is developed without slowing down the transition from eccentric to concentric work by the muscles involved\" (PDF p. 285 / printed p. 274). - \"The optimal height of a depth jump does not result in a landing where the heel is forced to the ground by momentum\" (PDF p. 287 / printed p. 276, item 8). - \"One should aim to increase the speed and acceleration of movement before increasing the drop height or take-off height\" (PDF p. 285 / \n\n### heuristic--depth-jump-weekly-frequency-and-seasonal-placement\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\nconfidence: contested\n**What it is** — From the authors' depth-jump recommendations (PDF pp. 286-287 / printed pp. 275-276, verified against the page images): - **Weekly frequency (item 3):** depth jumps \"in the volume indicated should be done once or twice a week in training sessions devoted to special strength training\", which can also include \"localised exercises for other muscle groups and general developmental exercises executed with a small volume.\" But \"Well-conditioned athletes can execute depth-jumps 3 times per week for 2 sets of 10 jumps at the completion of technical training in the particular sport.\" - **Spacing from technique (item 4):** \"Depth-jumps strongly excite the nervous system. Therefore, th\n\n### heuristic--dynamic-correspondence-means-matching-motor-characteristics-not-copying-the-sport-movement\nchapter: \"5.1\"\nconfidence: supported\n**What it is** — The authors warn of \"an excessive passion for making the strength training means correspond structurally to the special exercises.\" The misinterpretation of dynamic correspondence \"lies in literally copying the special exercise in training movements, instead of selecting a specific arsenal of means corresponding to the most important motor characteristics of the given sport and controlling their growth by creating conditions under which they will develop steadily.\" In their words, \"it is inappropriate to try to simulate sports specific movements with supplementary training activities,\" because this \"not only does ... not accurately develop the necessary fitness and motor abi\n\n### heuristic--early-specialisation-by-motor-regime-not-by-sport\nchapter: \"5.4.1\"\nconfidence: untested\n**What it is** — The authors apply the convergence principle to youth: \"particular consideration was given to the advisability of early specialisation, not in the specific type of sport, but in the specific motor regime involved\" (Verkhoshansky 1963, 1972 cited). \"orientation of the child to the sport and physical training with the sports means does not begin with specialised exercise to achieve competitive performance standards (which can easily lead to forced training). Instead, it begins with systematic management of the functional perfecting of the body\" (PDF p. 306 / printed p. 295, checked against the page image). The model has two stages: 1. A child who shows \"a predisposition to a pa\n\n### heuristic--equip-circuits-with-free-weights-and-pulleys-over-multistation-machines\nchapter: \"5.6\"\nconfidence: supported\n**What it is** — For circuits, the authors rank equipment by cost and function. Where cost matters, \"specialised variable and non-variable multi-purpose machines have to be regarded as the lowest priority,\" and \"a few inexpensive 'hi-lo' pulley machines would be a more than adequate substitute. In fact, the latter offer a level of three-dimensional variety that other machines cannot emulate.\" \"A well chosen collection of free weights, benches, racks and pulley machines, wedded with a skipping surface, padded aerobics area, bouncing boards and a few basic fixed cycles, offers virtually everything that the most impressive multi-station machines offer at a fraction of the price.\" \"Free weights \n\n### heuristic--isometric-apre-six-second-maximum-protocol\nchapter: \"5.2.2\"\nconfidence: supported\n**What it is** — APRE \"may also be applied with an isometric training regime and may be especially valuable during the earliest stages of muscular rehabilitation, when dynamic activities may be inadvisable.\" The authors base the dose on Ivanov's data: \"the optimal combination of these variables is 6 repetitions of 100% of one's 1RM held for 6-8 seconds per repetition (Figs 5.2, 5.3)\" (PDF p. 274 / printed p. 263). The protocol: hold a weight isometrically \"for a 6SM (6 second maximum) at a specified joint angle (beginning at or near full extension in most cases), with a 5-10 second rest between repetitions.\" Set 1 at 50% of the 6SM weight, set 2 at 75%, set 3 \"until the weight cannot be held\n\n### heuristic--plyometric-training-coupling-under-0-15-s-feedforward-and-force-time-pattern\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\nconfidence: supported\n**What it is** — The authors give three tests for whether a drill is classical \"training plyometrics\" rather than ordinary jumping: 1. **Coupling time.** The method \"is characterised by impulsive action of minimal duration between the end of the eccentric braking phase and initiation of the concentric acceleration phase\". \"If the transition phase (or, coupling phase) is prolonged by more than about 0.15 second, the action may be considered to constitute ordinary jumping and not classical training plyometrics.\" The coaching image: imagine the contact surface \"is red hot, so that any prolonged contact would be dangerous.\" (PDF p. 279 / printed p. 268, verified against the page image.) 2. **Fee\n\n### heuristic--rate-plyometric-intensity-by-contact-momentum-from-drop-height\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\nconfidence: supported\n**What it is** — The text (Siff, using his term \"powermetrics\", PDF p. 279 / printed p. 268) proposes \"a *powermetrics intensity scale* similar to that used in weightlifting, where exercises are ranked in terms of percentages of one's 1RM.\" Because \"the concept of 1RMs is meaningless in plyometric training\", one must use estimates of muscle tension or of \"the impulse ... produced during the contact phase\". \"Impulse is equivalent to the change of momentum (m.ΔV) during the amortisation phase, so that its magnitude for a falling body depends on the height from which the body drops. Thus, the momentum just before contact (estimated from height of the drop jump or distance fallen by the object) \n\n### heuristic--replace-means-as-their-training-effect-fades\nchapter: \"5.4.4\"\nconfidence: supported\n**What it is** — \"Preservation of the training effect means that the load should always produce a training effect by the systematic, timely introduction of more effective training means based on logical continuity,\" to avoid \"the states of stagnation and deterioration described in Chapter 1 (see Fig 1.32).\" In practice, \"as the training effect of some means decreases, so more effective, newer means are introduced which, in turn, are replaced by even more effective means.\" This needs \"experimental assessment of the training effect and categorisation of the specific complex of means\" (PDF p. 308 / printed p. 297). Fig 5.18 (verified against the page image) gives the authors' example for \"devel\n\n### heuristic--speed-strength-loading-light-loads-maximal-acceleration-and-5-to-1-mix\nchapter: \"5.2.3\"\nconfidence: supported\n**What it is** — \"The development of speed-strength is more effective the more speed loading there is in training and the shorter the time spent using slow movements (Yakovlev et al, 1960).\" \"Exercise with small loads (with approximately 20% of 1RM) is the principal method of developing speed-strength\" (Korobkov 1953; Vasiliev 1954; Gerasimov, Yakhontov 1954; Zimkin 1956; Agdgomelashvili 1964; Butenko 1967) (PDF p. 275-276 / printed p. 264-265, verified against the page images). With it, \"speed of movement increases with and without loads, and experiments have shown that the general increase in speed can reach 146% of the initial level.\" \"Movements should be executed with maximal effort and \n\n### heuristic--strength-endurance-load-tempo-and-the-75-80-percent-ceiling\nchapter: \"5.2.5\"\nconfidence: contested\n**What it is** — Section 5.2.5 (PDF pp. 296-298 / printed pp. 285-287; pp. 296-298 verified against the page images): - **Eight determinants.** Training for strength-endurance \"is determined predominantly by: 1. The magnitude of the load 2. The tempo of movement 3. The duration of each movement and each phase of the movements 4. The rest interval between training sessions 5. The length of the training period 6. The initial levels of strength-endurance of the athlete 7. The kinesiological structure of the movement 8. The relative involvement of static and dynamic strength-endurance\" (PDF p. 296 / printed p. 285). - **Basic prescription.** \"Repetition work with weights 25-50% of 1RM at a moder\n\n### heuristic--strength-endurance-under-demanding-sport-like-conditions\nchapter: \"5.2.5\"\nconfidence: supported\n**What it is** — \"For developing strength-endurance it is always appropriate to strive to execute the work under demanding conditions, though with movements whose coordination and structure in space and time are close to those of the special exercise. For instance, a runner may run with a small parachute resisting him or pull a loaded sled in the preparatory phases (Popov, 1966). Similarly, gymnasts may use a heavy belt and vest (Zhilei, 1964) and rowers a special water-resistance device attached to the hull of the boat (Chuprun, 1966). In the case of the rowers, strength-endurance as measured by the number of pull-ups, the number of arm flexions in the prone position and the number of barbe\n\n### principle--converging-partial-effects-of-strength-training-means\nchapter: \"5.4.1\"\nconfidence: supported\n**What it is** — \"The principle of converging the partial effects of strength training means is fundamental to the long-term organisation of strength training. It is achieved by ... the gradual convergence of their cumulative training effect to those important specific characteristics which are essential to the functioning of the body in a particular sport\" (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294). Early in the sports training process (STP), \"the tasks which determine the diversity of the training means are far apart,\" linked \"only by the long-term plan.\" Specialisation then \"is firstly according to ability (but not to the physiological system) and is not related to the motor pattern (see 2.2.3).\" \"As \n\n### principle--sequentially-introduce-means-of-greater-training-effect\nchapter: \"5.3.2\"\nconfidence: supported\n**What it is** — In 5.3.2, \"A Sequential System of Training Means,\" the authors argue that the development of a specific motor ability \"occurs in a specific pattern,\" so \"it is necessary to sequentially improve the functioning of those systems and physiological mechanisms which are crucial for developing a specific motor ability\" (PDF p. 302 / printed p. 291). \"Progressive functional changes in some systems act as prerequisites for improving the performance of other systems.\" The rule: \"the means used should elicit a definite training effect, but its magnitude should correspond to the body's current level of specific fitness and increase in conjunction with the latter.\" Hence \"the necessity \n\n### principle--speed-strength-strength-endurance-and-speed-endurance-are-independent-qualities\nchapter: \"5.1\"\nconfidence: supported\n**What it is** — The authors state that \"speed-strength, strength-endurance and speed-endurance are not simply derivatives of strength, speed and endurance, but are totally independent qualities.\" Their evidence is that \"an increase in absolute strength does not necessarily enhance any of these three qualities.\" So the combined qualities \"should be placed alongside the latter in a group, each with its own particular means and methods of development (Verkhoshansky, 1963)\" (PDF p. 268 / printed p. 257). The same paragraph frames force and velocity as qualities that, \"at a high level of development are inversely proportional to one another (see Ch 4),\" and says excessive development of both \"is\n\n","numLines":231,"startLine":1,"totalLines":231}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"7f8742b4-2e77-4a14-af2e-f734023be81c","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
