{"parentUuid":"c112080f-453f-4e33-8932-eaf6878c767e","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f3859be2-3513-4b8e-bb88-ac5e3cefe2da","agentId":"a8e36db408996cf67","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018wYyu5B15SgLsT6wkb2vBD","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\n2\t##### principle--force-time-profile-determines-strength-vs-power-training-target  ->  claim--newton-ii-and-impulse-momentum-as-the-math-behind-the-force-time-curve\n3\tSRC TITLE: - | chapter: \"2.2.2\"\n4\tSRC RELATED: **Related** — [[framework--force-time-curve-adaptation-pattern-by-movement-type]], [[heuristic--isometric-dynamometry-misrepresents-dynamic-functional-strength]], [[heuristic--strength-deficit-sets-training-method-choice]] (Chapter 1). \n5\t  TGT CITES: exerts more force than B at any instant before time T2, so A has the advantage where \"rapid RFD\" is needed (jumping, striking, throwing), while B reaches the higher peak and sustains force longer, an advantage in \"wrestling, powerlifting and scrummaging in rugby\" (PDF p. 267 / printed p. 256). This comparison is already atomized from Ch 2.2.2 in [[principle--force-time-profile-determines-strength-vs-power-training-target]].  **Related** — [[principle--force-time-profile-determines-strength-vs-power-training-target]] (Fig 5.1 repeats the Ch 2 two-athlete comparison); [[heuristic--rfd-as-correspondence-criterion-and-impulse-cannot-compare-movements]] (4.3.4, the autho\n6\t  TGT CITES: \" is needed (jumping, striking, throwing), while B reaches the higher peak and sustains force longer, an advantage in \"wrestling, powerlifting and scrummaging in rugby\" (PDF p. 267 / printed p. 256). This comparison is already atomized from Ch 2.2.2 in [[principle--force-time-profile-determines-strength-vs-power-training-target]].  **Related** — [[principle--force-time-profile-determines-strength-vs-power-training-target]] (Fig 5.1 repeats the Ch 2 two-athlete comparison); [[heuristic--rfd-as-correspondence-criterion-and-impulse-cannot-compare-movements]] (4.3.4, the authors' caution on comparing impulse across movements); [[claim--average-or-peak-force-times-veloci\n7\t\n8\t##### claim--strength-detraining-and-retention-timelines  ->  framework--three-methods-for-maximum-strength-repetitive-brief-maximal-isometric\n9\tSRC TITLE: - | chapter: \"4.1.1\"\n10\tSRC RELATED: **Related** — [[claim--adaptive-reconstruction-vs-supercompensation]], [[claim--ligament-tendon-adaptation-and-detraining-response]], [[claim--delayed-strength-training-effect-lag-before-gains-appear]], [[block-periodization--issurin/claim--residual-training-effect-durations-by-motor-ability]], [[elite-sports-performance--evans/framework--three-types-of-overload-stimulating-retaining-detraining]]; [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--kots-electrostimulation-strength-claims-not-replicated]]: Ch4.2.1, Kots/Khvilon electrostimulation strength claims and the book's \"established\" vs \"continue to disagree\" \n11\t  TGT CITES: maximal weights \"enhances the ability to accelerate heavy loads\" (PDF p. 271 / printed p. 260). They also note that strength \"is retained for longer if it is founded first upon a base of increased muscle hypertrophy\" and declines faster if built mainly on neuromuscular efficiency (PDF p. 269 / printed p. 258); that retention point is atomized in [[claim--strength-detraining-and-retention-timelines]].  **When to apply** — Eric's baseline: \"No sport performs true 1RM testing. Maxes are projected from observation plus the athlete's history\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 61, Explicit, Q10), and %1RM prescription is kept \"stated explicitly as bu\n12\t  TGT CITES: for 6-10 sets of 1-3 repetitions in one training session (Medvedev, 1967; Vorobyev, 1971)\" (PDF p. 271 / printed p. 260).  **Related** — [[claim--delorme-oxford-mccloy-progressive-resistance-variants-compared]]; [[claim--berger-sets-reps-and-frequency-studies-in-novices]]; [[claim--ivanov-1966-contraction-mode-and-which-strength-test-improves]]; [[claim--strength-detraining-and-retention-timelines]]; [[framework--types-of-maximal-strength]]; [[framework--sarcomere-vs-sarcoplasmic-hypertrophy]]; [[heuristic--isometric-training-execution-protocol-and-advantages-drawbacks]] \n13\t\n14\t##### claim--strength-training-threshold-stimulus  ->  claim--moderate-and-mixed-movement-tempo-for-repetition-strength-gains\n15\tSRC TITLE: - | chapter: \"1.2\"\n16\tSRC RELATED: **Related** — [[heuristic--program-variable-complexity-scales-with-training-age]], [[periodization--bompa/principle--progressive-overload-threshold]]; [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--hettinger-muller-isometric-strength-gain-rate]]: Ch4.2.4, where the book says duration, not magnitude, is the fundamental isometric conditioning factor, in tension with this threshold rule \n17\t  TGT CITES: o the 22.6 versus 16.3 kg comparison cannot be scaled to another lift. The sentence order is also loose: the authors first say the \"largest increases\" come from moderate tempo, then that a combination of tempos is \"most effective\"; the numbers support the second. The equal-work part of Vasiliev (1954) overlaps the uniform-gain finding already in [[claim--strength-training-threshold-stimulus]]; only the tempo detail is new here. Inference: do not read \"high tempo was not very effective for developing strength\" as \"do not lift fast\"; it concerns maximal strength gain from the repetition method, not speed-strength, which 5.2.3 trains with\n18\t  TGT CITES: \" as \"do not lift fast\"; it concerns maximal strength gain from the repetition method, not speed-strength, which 5.2.3 trains with light loads at maximal speed.  **Example** — The authors' own figures: Lelikov (1975), 10 weeks, mixed tempos 22.6 ± 0.6 kg against 16.3 ± 0.5 kg for moderate tempo alone (PDF p. 271 / printed p. 260).  **Related** — [[claim--strength-training-threshold-stimulus]]; [[framework--three-methods-for-maximum-strength-repetitive-brief-maximal-isometric]]; [[framework--compensatory-acceleration-training]]; [[heuristic--speed-strength-loading-light-loads-maximal-acceleration-and-5-to-1-mix]] (5.2.3, where fast temp\n19\t\n20\t##### framework--two-force-regimes-overcoming-weight-vs-inertia  ->  heuristic--speed-strength-loading-light-loads-maximal-acceleration-and-5-to-1-mix\n21\tSRC TITLE: - | chapter: \"4.2.2\"\n22\tSRC RELATED: **Related** — [[claim--power-output-peaks-then-declines-with-load-jump-vs-leg-press]], [[framework--compensatory-acceleration-training]] (Ch1, a related point that free-weight resistance already varies with acceleration per Newton's second law); [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--inertial-flywheel-and-elastic-band-resistance-devices]]: the book's two inertial-training solutions, p.219; the inertial entry already links back \n23\t  TGT CITES: an effect size. The \"5:1\" ratio has no cited source on the page. \"Alternation ... is most effective for the ranked athlete\" gives no data or definition of \"ranked.\" Percentages of 1RM rest on a 1RM the department does not test (line 61, Explicit, Q10), so the 20% and 40% loads are percentages of a projected max. The inertia method is atomized in [[framework--two-force-regimes-overcoming-weight-vs-inertia]]; the authors themselves say they cannot give \"specific recommendations for its application.\"  **Example** — The authors' own summary prescription for brief acyclic actions: mainly 20% of 1RM, combined with loads up to 40% of 1RM in a ratio of 5 to\n24\t  TGT CITES:  they cannot give \"specific recommendations for its application.\"  **Example** — The authors' own summary prescription for brief acyclic actions: mainly 20% of 1RM, combined with loads up to 40% of 1RM in a ratio of 5 to 1 (PDF p. 278 / printed p. 267).  **Related** — [[framework--two-groups-of-speed-strength-movements-and-their-tension-types]]; [[framework--two-force-regimes-overcoming-weight-vs-inertia]]; [[claim--power-output-peaks-then-declines-with-load-jump-vs-leg-press]]; [[framework--compensatory-acceleration-training]]; [[claim--implement-mass-for-speed-strength-keep-technique-and-size-per-athlete]]; [[heuristic--cyclic-vs-acyclic-speed-str\n25\t\n26\t##### framework--post-activation-potentiation-load-timing-sequencing  ->  case-study--arm-throw-height-after-barbell-press-tonic-work\n27\tSRC TITLE: - | chapter: \"3.4.2\"\n28\tSRC RELATED: **Related** — [[framework--warmup-mechanisms-and-task-specific-guidelines]] (the preceding subsection on general warm-up, a related but distinct CNS-priming phenomenon); [[claim--pap-findings-contested-western-vs-russian-literature]] (the documented Western/Russian disagreement on whether this effect reliably occurs at all); [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--acute-delayed-particular-cumulative-training-effect-taxonomy]]: the book's training-effect taxonomy, whose worked example (squat before sprint can potentiate or depress) links back to this PAP entry; [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--non-commutative-non-associative-training-order]]: the book's general rule that training order changes the effect, of which PAP sequencing is one case \n29\t  TGT CITES: preliminary throws with a heavier shot (Bischke & Morehouse 1950); see [[claim--pap-findings-contested-western-vs-russian-literature]].  **Example** — The authors' own case: after 3 x 3 barbell presses at 80% of 1RM and 10 minutes' rest, the throw height rose from 44.0 to 61.0 cm in Table 5.3 (PDF p. 276-277 / printed p. 265-266).  **Related** — [[framework--post-activation-potentiation-load-timing-sequencing]] (3.4.2, the after-effect rules); [[claim--pap-findings-contested-western-vs-russian-literature]]; [[claim--thrown-load-height-decreases-with-load-increases-with-drop-height]]; [[claim--average-or-peak-force-times-velocity-is-not-power]]; [[heurist\n30\t\n31\t##### claim--pap-findings-contested-western-vs-russian-literature  ->  case-study--arm-throw-height-after-barbell-press-tonic-work\n32\tSRC TITLE: - | chapter: \"3.4.2\"\n33\tSRC RELATED: *