no weightings. \"60% of the maximal work capacity time\" is not defined further (Inference: 60% of the time to failure is the plain reading).  **Example** — The authors' own: 25-50% 1RM at 60-120 reps per minute to fatigue, or to 60% of the maximal work time (PDF p. 296 / printed p. 285).  **Related** — [[framework--strength-endurance-taxonomy-and-absolute-vs-partial]] (Ch3.2.2, the dynamic/static and absolute/partial distinctions this section restates); [[claim--zatsiorsky-and-hansen-show-strength-endurance-training-specificity]]; [[case-study--soviet-sport-specific-strength-endurance-prescriptions]]; [[framewo\n\n##### claim--strength-training-raises-haemoglobin-myoglobin-and-aids-distance-sport  ->  claim--strength-endurance-hypertrophy-and-cyclic-endurance\nSRC TITLE: - | chapter: \"4.4\"\nSRC RELATED: **Related** — [[claim--endurance-rests-on-muscle-oxidative-and-strength-qualities-not-vo2max]], [[principle--motor-system-leads-cardiorespiratory-adaptation-do-not-separate-them]], [[claim--continuous-vs-interval-circuit-training-for-concurrent-goals]] (Ch1.12, endurance work compromising strength), [[block-periodization--issurin/case-study--concurrent-training-interference-and-decay]] (Issurin, interference in the other direction). \n  TGT CITES: uld the training ever produce a decrease in relative strength of any muscle groups in the body\" (PDF p. 298 / printed p. 287).  **Tension with Chapter 4, recorded:** section 4.4 cites research that strength training \"contributes more to the largest improvements in distance sports than aerobic training\" (PDF pp. 261-262 / printed pp. 250-251; see [[claim--strength-training-raises-haemoglobin-myoglobin-and-aids-distance-sport]]), while 5.2.5 says squats, presses and good mornings \"will even hinder the development of endurance in cyclic type exercises\" (PDF p. 297 / printed p. 286). Inference: the two may differ in which strength exercises and loads are meant, but the boo\n  TGT CITES: ngth-endurance-prescriptions]]), which is itself in tension with the Maisuradze sentence.  **Example** — The authors' own: gymnasts, R = 0.77 between strength-endurance and relative strength (Zhalei, 1964), against middle-distance runners, skaters and skiers with no definite strength-mass correlation (PDF p. 297 / printed p. 286).  **Related** — [[claim--strength-training-raises-haemoglobin-myoglobin-and-aids-distance-sport]]; [[claim--endurance-rests-on-muscle-oxidative-and-strength-qualities-not-vo2max]]; [[framework--bodymass-strength-scaling-laws]]; [[heuristic--strength-endurance-load-tempo-and-the-75-80-percent-ceiling]] \n\n##### principle--equal-force-different-loading-methods  ->  claim--heavy-squats-and-light-fast-lifts-miss-the-eccentric-concentric-switch\nSRC TITLE: - | chapter: \"1.2\"\nSRC RELATED: **Related** — [[principle--force-velocity-relationship-and-training-specificity]]. \n  TGT CITES: athlete bodymass or level, so it is not a prescription.  **Example** — The authors' own example: heavy squats (slow, quasi-isometric) versus squat thrusts with a 60 kg barbell (large dynamic maximum force), with neither training the rapid switch (PDF p. 278 / printed p. 267).  **Related** — [[framework--shock-method-and-plyometric-terminology]]; [[principle--equal-force-different-loading-methods]]; [[heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size]]; [[principle--rate-of-force-development-as-independent-limiter]] \n\n##### claim--reversible-muscular-action-is-a-trainable-skill-not-automatic  ->  heuristic--plyometric-training-coupling-under-0-15-s-feedforward-and-force-time-pattern\nSRC TITLE: - | chapter: \"2\"\nSRC RELATED: **Related** — [[framework--stretch-shortening-cycle-mechanisms-and-tendon-elastic-storage]], [[claim--eccentric-force-exceeds-concentric-and-isometric-force]], [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--strength-deficit-jump-comparison-estimate]]. \n  TGT CITES: he original definition and \"powermetrics\"); [[principle--short-amortization-preserves-elastic-energy]]; [[framework--five-phases-of-a-plyometric-action]]; [[claim--prestretch-benefit-decay-time-differs-by-task]]; [[framework--plyometric-training-classification-impact-intensity-and-function]]; [[science-and-practice-of-strength-training--zatsiorsky/claim--reversible-muscular-action-is-a-trainable-skill-not-automatic]] \n\n##### claim--reversible-muscular-action-is-a-trainable-skill-not-automatic  ->  claim--plyometric-skill-is-cognitive-and-state-dependent\n  TGT CITES: afterwards modifying them (PDF p. 295 / printed p. 284).  **Related** — [[framework--kinaesthetic-system-and-feedforward-visualization]]; [[heuristic--plyometric-training-coupling-under-0-15-s-feedforward-and-force-time-pattern]]; [[claim--hypnosis-and-social-presence-alter-strength-output]]; [[science-and-practice-of-strength-training--zatsiorsky/claim--reversible-muscular-action-is-a-trainable-skill-not-automatic]] \n\n##### claim--residual-training-effect-durations-by-motor-ability  ->  heuristic--depth-jump-weekly-frequency-and-seasonal-placement\nSRC TITLE: - | chapter: \"1.2.1\"\nSRC RELATED: **Related** — [[framework--accumulation-transmutation-realization-mesocycles]], [[periodization--bompa/heuristic--fatigue-fitness-3to1-ratio]] (a ratio-based rule for fatigue-vs-fitness decay, distinct from this table's absolute per-ability day counts: cross-reference, do not conflate), [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--fitness-fatigue-modeling-and-taper-timing]] (staging), [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--heterochronic-biochemical-adaptation-sequence]] (staging); [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--strength-detraining-and-retention-timelines]]: Supertraining's strength detraining and retention timelines, which already link back to this entry; [[claim--alactic-detraining-if-stimulated-only-on-game-day]] (SSAC talk: If the Alactic System Is Stimulated Only on Saturday, It May Detrain: A Five-to-Eight-Day Rule of Thumb) \n  TGT CITES: ne at least 10 days out (PDF p. 286 / printed p. 275).  **Related** — [[heuristic--maximal-plyometric-and-depth-jump-volume-and-rest]]; [[claim--delayed-strength-training-effect-lag-before-gains-appear]] (Ch4.1, the delayed training effect); [[framework--acute-delayed-particular-cumulative-training-effect-taxonomy]]; [[block-periodization--issurin/claim--residual-training-effect-durations-by-motor-ability]] \n\n##### principle--concentration-of-training-workloads  ->  framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means\nSRC TITLE: - | chapter: \"1.2.2\"\nSRC RELATED: **Related** — [[framework--accumulation-transmutation-realization-mesocycles]], the three-block structure this principle produces. [[periodization--bompa/principle--concentrated-loading-overreaching]] and [[periodization--bompa/framework--step-loading-paradigm]], converging concentrated-load ideas already in this library. [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/principle--specificity-of-training-ten-dimensions]] (staging): check for overlap on why generalized simultaneous training under-delivers for advanced athletes. \n  TGT CITES: dominated by a narrow target quality and restricted where many interacting abilities decide performance (see [[research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]]). Issurin 2008 agrees with the authors that concentration is for high-level athletes ([[block-periodization--issurin/principle--concentration-of-training-workloads]], PDF 44 / printed 33), so the dispute is over which sports the unidirectional version suits, not over concentration as such. Note also a naming overlap in the text: \"complex system\" is used for both the concurrent system the authors reject for eli\n  TGT CITES: -a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]], [[principle--sequentially-introduce-means-of-greater-training-effect]], [[heuristic--replace-means-as-their-training-effect-fades]], [[framework--sequential-and-concurrent-variants-of-systematic-means]], [[framework--four-principles-of-spe