ts-of-systematic-means]], [[heuristic--sequence-jumps-then-barbell-then-depth-jumps-across-stages]] (the conclusions the authors draw), [[framework--post-activation-potentiation-load-timing-sequencing]] (the after-effect within a session), [[claim--pap-findings-contested-western-vs-russian-literature]], [[framework--non-commutative-non-associative-training-order]], [[block-periodization--issurin/heuristic--exercise-sequencing-by-fatigue-state]], [[claim--depth-jump-training-beat-higher-volume-traditional-training-in-jumpers]] (5.2.4, the resistance-before-plyometrics rule), [[heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size]] (5.2.4, whose resistance-first rule for combined sessions these novice results do not support). \n  TGT CITES: r track-and-field jumpers in a preparation phase. The resistance-first rule is not supported for novices: in a within-workout experiment on novices (strength then speed-strength work versus the reverse), \"There was no statistically significant difference in speed-strength of the subjects in the first two groups\" (PDF p. 299 / printed p. 288; see [[case-study--novice-strength-and-speed-strength-sequencing-experiments]]). The authors expect the strength-first effect to hold for well-trained athletes, but say \"the validity of this hypothesis can be established conclusively only by careful experimentation\" (same page).  **Example** — The authors' own experiment as \n  TGT CITES: oted above (PDF p. 288 / printed p. 277).  **Related** — [[heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size]]; [[heuristic--maximal-plyometric-and-depth-jump-volume-and-rest]]; [[heuristic--structured-load-progression-beats-volume-alone-or-random-combination]]; [[framework--post-activation-potentiation-load-timing-sequencing]]; [[case-study--novice-strength-and-speed-strength-sequencing-experiments]] (Ch5.3, novice sequencing) \n\n##### case-study--novice-strength-and-speed-strength-sequencing-experiments  ->  heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size\n  TGT CITES: ed sessions is not supported for novices: in a within-workout experiment on novices (strength then speed-strength work versus the reverse, pauses lengthened slightly), \"There was no statistically significant difference in speed-strength of the subjects in the first two groups\", and a depth-jump group gained most (PDF p. 299 / printed p. 288; see [[case-study--novice-strength-and-speed-strength-sequencing-experiments]]). The same page reports a separate across-stage experiment in which weights first, then depth jumps, was more effective, and the authors expect the within-session effect to hold for well-trained athletes, but \"the validity of this hypothesis can b\n  TGT CITES: strength-deficit-sets-training-method-choice]]; [[framework--strength-deficit-jump-comparison-estimate]]; [[claim--high-volume-plyometrics-do-not-build-rebound-fatigue-resistance]]; [[claim--depth-jump-training-beat-higher-volume-traditional-training-in-jumpers]]; [[claim--heavy-squats-and-light-fast-lifts-miss-the-eccentric-concentric-switch]]; [[case-study--novice-strength-and-speed-strength-sequencing-experiments]] (Ch5.3, novice sequencing) \n\n##### heuristic--analytical-synthetic-and-multifaceted-preparation-suit-only-early-training  ->  heuristic--choose-cross-training-by-fitness-qualities-and-phase-not-sport-label\nSRC TITLE: - | chapter: \"5.1\"\nSRC RELATED: **Related** — [[claim--physical-quality-transfer-decreases-as-specific-fitness-increases]]; [[principle--speed-strength-strength-endurance-and-speed-endurance-are-independent-qualities]]; [[framework--non-commutative-non-associative-training-order]] (sequencing of means); [[block-periodization--issurin/principle--training-modality-compatibility-in-a-workout]]; [[periodization--bompa/framework--ltad-sequential-model]] \n\n##### heuristic--choose-cross-training-by-fitness-qualities-and-phase-not-sport-label  ->  heuristic--analytical-synthetic-and-multifaceted-preparation-suit-only-early-training\nSRC TITLE: - | chapter: \"5.5\"\nSRC RELATED: **Related** — [[heuristic--no-supplementary-sport-is-categorically-good-or-bad]], [[framework--sequential-and-concurrent-variants-of-systematic-means]], [[framework--specific-correspondence-of-the-training-effect-four-tasks]] (specialisation by system and by ability), [[claim--irradiation-effect-raises-prime-mover-force-and-endurance]] (the contralateral \"cross-training\" effect), [[framework--functional-vs-nonfunctional-resistance-machines]] (the machine limits the authors repeat for cross training, PDF p. 311), [[skill-acquisition/enhancing-skill--myszka/framework--choosing-a-donor-sport]] (Myszka chooses donor sports by shared movements and affordances, a skill-acquisition angle on the same selection question) \n\n##### principle--speed-strength-strength-endurance-and-speed-endurance-are-independent-qualities  ->  framework--four-principles-of-special-strength-training-organisation\nSRC TITLE: - | chapter: \"5.1\"\nSRC RELATED: **Related** — [[framework--strength-speed-continuum-five-zones]]; [[claim--physical-quality-transfer-decreases-as-specific-fitness-increases]]; [[framework--motor-ability-connection-types-taxonomy]]; [[framework--special-strength-methods-classified-by-sport-group-and-strength-type]] (5.2, the method groups that follow from this split) \n\n##### framework--four-principles-of-special-strength-training-organisation  ->  principle--speed-strength-strength-endurance-and-speed-endurance-are-independent-qualities\nSRC TITLE: - | chapter: \"5.4\"\nSRC RELATED: **Related** — [[principle--converging-partial-effects-of-strength-training-means]], [[principle--accelerating-specific-adaptation]], [[framework--specific-correspondence-of-the-training-effect-four-tasks]], [[heuristic--replace-means-as-their-training-effect-fades]], [[claim--physical-quality-transfer-decreases-as-specific-fitness-increases]], [[framework--five-criteria-of-dynamic-correspondence]], [[framework--c