{"parentUuid":"4072a463-261c-4eb4-b2f2-f31765df6dd8","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f3859be2-3513-4b8e-bb88-ac5e3cefe2da","agentId":"a8e36db408996cf67","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TN9ZUz46psX48QcYbPBLhg","type":"tool_result","content":"---\n---\n**What it is** — Reactive ability (the neuromuscular capacity to generate explosive force\nvia a preliminary stretch immediately before concentric work) is quantified with a\n**Reactivity Coefficient**, which the text defines three ways without fully reconciling\nthem: earlier (2.2.1, printed 108) as \"the explosive strength index relative to body\nweight,\" RC = Fmax/(tmax.W) = RFDmax/W; here, the lab-only form RC = K2/K1 (a ratio of two\ncharacteristics of muscular work, e.g. power, before and after the stretch), and the\nfield-practical form **RC = Fmax / (tmax x m x g)**, where Fmax is peak force, tmax is time\nto reach it, m is the moving mass (or bodymass), and g is gravitational acceleration. Despite\nthat formula, the text separately states that in the reactivity test itself, \"the\ncoefficient of reactivity was determined from the length of the support period and analysis\nof the mean take-off force\" (printed 137), not a literal Fmax/tmax calculation; the text\ndoes not explain how the two are reconciled. Verkhoshansky's own \"reactivity test\" protocol:","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"8e9bbd8c-9f2c-40c6-870b-163ced2e12d4","timestamp":"2026-10-03T17:06:42.562Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"---\n---\n**What it is** — Reactive ability (the neuromuscular capacity to generate explosive force\nvia a preliminary stretch immediately before concentric work) is quantified with a\n**Reactivity Coefficient**, which the text defines three ways without fully reconciling\nthem: earlier (2.2.1, printed 108) as \"the explosive strength index relative to body\nweight,\" RC = Fmax/(tmax.W) = RFDmax/W; here, the lab-only form RC = K2/K1 (a ratio of two\ncharacteristics of muscular work, e.g. power, before and after the stretch), and the\nfield-practical form **RC = Fmax / (tmax x m x g)**, where Fmax is peak force, tmax is time\nto reach it, m is the moving mass (or bodymass), and g is gravitational acceleration. Despite\nthat formula, the text separately states that in the reactivity test itself, \"the\ncoefficient of reactivity was determined from the length of the support period and analysis\nof the mean take-off force\" (printed 137), not a literal Fmax/tmax calculation; the text\ndoes not explain how the two are reconciled. Verkhoshansky's own \"reactivity test\" protocol:","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"4072a463-261c-4eb4-b2f2-f31765df6dd8","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
