d -n '/^\\*\\*What it is/,/^\\*\\*Why/p' framework--five-phases-of-a-plyometric-action.md | cut -c1-1500 | head -20; grep -c 'foundation of plyometric training (5.2.4)' framework--four-mechanisms-of-stimulating-muscular-tension.md; grep -c 'see 5.2.2)' heuristic--isokinetic-testing-limitations-for-functional-sport-specificity.md; grep -n '^\\*\\*' heuristic--isokinetic-testing-limitations-for-functional-sport-specificity.md framework--four-mechanisms-of-stimulating-muscular-tension.md | cut -c1-120","description":"Read five-phases entry and confirm placeholder uniqueness"}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011Cfffq5qjP71PpBbW97ncb","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"5c5684db-03e1-41f3-ae75-69381bc43cf6","timestamp":"2026-10-03T17:07:19.704Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"5c5684db-03e1-41f3-ae75-69381bc43cf6","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f3859be2-3513-4b8e-bb88-ac5e3cefe2da","agentId":"a8e36db408996cf67","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01T3BAPeSbNv5XeHG43vbBX8","type":"tool_result","content":"**What it is** — Under \"The Fundamental Theory of Plyometrics\" the authors list five phases (PDF p. 281 / printed p. 270; Fig 5.5 verified against the page image):\n1. **Initial momentum phase:** the body or limb moves \"because of kinetic energy (KE) it has accumulated from a preceding action.\"\n2. **Electromechanical delay phase:** contact with a surface stops the limb and \"provokes the muscles to contract\"; the delay is \"the time elapsing between the onset of the action potential in the motor nerves and the onset of the muscle contraction.\" The authors note some workers include lengthening of the SEC (series elastic component) in this phase, but \"electromechanical\" was coined for the electrical-to-mechanical interval, \"which does not include the passive stretching of connective tissue.\"\n3. **Amortisation phase:** the KE \"produces a powerful myotatic stretch reflex which leads to eccentric muscle contraction accompanied by explosive isometric contraction\" and stretching of the SEC (e.g. tendons). \"The explosive isometric phase between the end of the eccentric action and the beginning of the concentric action lasts for a period known as the *coupling time*.\"\n4. **Rebound phase:** release of elastic energy from the SEC, with involuntary concentric contraction evoked by the stretch reflex, \"as well as possible local change in the muscle fibre mechanics\"; it \"may include the contribution added by voluntary concentric contraction.\"\n5. **Final momentum phase:** after the concentric contraction, the body or limb continues on the KE imparted by contraction and elastic recoil.\n\nFig 5.5, as drawn: boxes for initial momentum, amortisation, rebound and final momentum phases; between them, bars for \"EM delay\", \"eccentric contraction\" and \"concentric contraction\", joined at the bottom by a box \"explosive isometrics\" labelled \"Coupling Time\".\n\nThe caption words the EM delay differently: \"Electromechanical delay between signal to terminate initial momentum phase and instant when eccentric contraction begins\" (caption), against \"between the onset of the action potential in the motor nerves and the onset of the muscle contraction\" (text item 2), both on PDF p. 281 / printed p. 270. Inference: both describe the electrical-to-mechanical interval.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' mechanism is in the phase descriptions: stretch reflex plus SEC elastic energy, released in the rebound. Elsewhere they add that the process is not only spinal reflex and elastic recoil (see [[claim--plyometric-skill-is-cognitive-and-state-dependent]]).\n1\n1\nheuristic--isokinetic-testing-limitations-for-functional-sport-specificity.md:16:**What it is** — Building on the physic\nheuristic--isokinetic-testing-limitations-for-functional-sport-specificity.md:68:**Why it works** — Each of the itemised\nheuristic--isokinetic-testing-limitations-for-functional-sport-specificity.md:76:**When to apply** — Use this itemised l\nheuristic--isokinetic-testing-limitations-for-functional-sport-specificity.md:89:**When NOT to apply** — Marked conteste\nheuristic--isokinetic-testing-limitations-for-functional-sport-specificity.md:102:**Example** — Inference: a therapist i\nheuristic--isokinetic-testing-limitations-for-functional-sport-specificity.md:108:**Related** — [[heuristic--isometric-d\nframework--four-mechanisms-of-stimulating-muscular-tension.md:16:**What it is** — External muscular force is a product o\nframework--four-mechanisms-of-stimulating-muscular-tension.md:40:**Why it works** — The authors' stated reason: afferent\nframework--four-mechanisms-of-stimulating-muscular-tension.md:49:**When to apply** — Inference: use this as an organizin\nframework--four-mechanisms-of-stimulating-muscular-tension.md:58:**When NOT to apply** — Inference: this is the book's o\nframework--four-mechanisms-of-stimulating-muscular-tension.md:64:**Example** — Inference: a coach comparing a paused bac\nframework--four-mechanisms-of-stimulating-muscular-tension.md:71:**Related** — [[framework--shock-method-and-plyometric-","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"131cb37c-02ba-4ab4-b231-768fd2f6cdcf","timestamp":"2026-10-03T17:07:19.988Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"**What it is** — Under \"The Fundamental Theory of Plyometrics\" the authors list five phases (PDF p. 281 / printed p. 270; Fig 5.5 verified against the page image):\n1. **Initial momentum phase:** the body or limb moves \"because of kinetic energy (KE) it has accumulated from a preceding action.\"\n2. **Electromechanical delay phase:** contact with a surface stops the limb and \"provokes the muscles to contract\"; the delay is \"the time elapsing between the onset of the action potential in the motor nerves and the onset of the muscle contraction.\" The authors note some workers include lengthening of the SEC (series elastic component) in this phase, but \"electromechanical\" was coined for the electrical-to-mechanical interval, \"which does not include the passive stretching of connective tissue.\"\n3. **Amortisation phase:** the KE \"produces a powerful myotatic stretch reflex which leads to eccentric muscle contraction accompanied by explosive isometric contraction\" and stretching of the SEC (e.g. tendons). \"The explosive isometric phase between the end of the eccentric action and the beginning of the concentric action lasts for a period known as the *coupling time*.\"\n4. **Rebound phase:** release of elastic energy from the SEC, with involuntary concentric contraction evoked by the stretch reflex, \"as well as possible local change in the muscle fibre mechanics\"; it \"may include the contribution added by voluntary concentric contraction.\"\n5. **Final momentum phase:** after the concentric contraction, the body or limb continues on the KE imparted by contraction and elastic recoil.\n\nFig 5.5, as drawn: boxes for initial momentum, amortisation, rebound and final momentum phases; between them, bars for \"EM delay\", \"eccentric contraction\" and \"concentric contraction\", joined at the bottom by a box \"explosive isometrics\" labelled \"Coupling Time\".\n\nThe caption words the EM delay differently: \"Electromechanical delay between signal to terminate initial momentum phase and instant when eccentric contraction begins\" (caption), against \"between the onset of the action potential in the motor nerves and the onset of the muscle contraction\" (text item 2), both on PDF p. 281 / printed p. 270. Inference: both describe the electrical-to-mechanical interval.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' mechanism is in the phase descriptions: stretch reflex plus SEC elastic energy, released in the rebound. Elsewhere they add that the process is not only spinal reflex and elastic recoil (see [[claim--plyometric-skill-is-cognitive-and-state-dependent]]).\n1\n1\nheuristic--isokinetic-testing-limitations-for-functional-sport-specificity.md:16:**What it is** — Building on the physic\nheuristic--isokinetic-testing-limitations-for-functional-sport-specificity.md:68:**Why it works** — Each of the itemised\nheuristic--isokinetic-testing-limitations-for-functional-sport-specificity.md:76:**When to apply** — Use this itemised l\nheuristic--isokinetic-testing-limitations-for-functional-sport-specificity.md:89:**When NOT to apply** — Marked conteste\nheuristic--isokinetic-testing-limitations-for-functional-sport-specificity.md:102:**Example** — Inference: a therapist i\nheuristic--isokinetic-testing-limitations-for-functional-sport-specificity.md:108:**Related** — [[heuristic--isometric-d\nframework--four-mechanisms-of-stimulating-muscular-tension.md:16:**What it is** — External muscular force is a product o\nframework--four-mechanisms-of-stimulating-muscular-tension.md:40:**Why it works** — The authors' stated reason: afferent\nframework--four-mechanisms-of-stimulating-muscular-tension.md:49:**When to apply** — Inference: use this as an organizin\nframework--four-mechanisms-of-stimulating-muscular-tension.md:58:**When NOT to apply** — Inference: this is the book's o\nframework--four-mechanisms-of-stimulating-muscular-tension.md:64:**Example** — Inference: a coach comparing a paused bac\nframework--four-mechanisms-of-stimulating-muscular-tension.md:71:**Related** — [[framework--shock-method-and-plyometric-","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"5c5684db-03e1-41f3-ae75-69381bc43cf6","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
