eneral means and then integrating them, which the authors limit to early training) | review_supertraining_ch5a_opus.md, \"Reciprocal-link suggestions\" |\n| supertraining--verkhoshansky/principle--speed-strength-strength-endurance-and-speed-endurance-are-independent-qualities.md | [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--four-principles-of-special-strength-training-organisation]] | vault + staging (identical); source and target both promoted 2026-10-03 | [[framework--four-principles-of-special-strength-training-organisation]] (5.4, the organising principles, which start from the point that links between abilities weaken as proficiency grows) | review_supertraining_ch5a_opus.md, \"Reciprocal-link suggestions\" |\n| supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--four-principles-of-special-strength-training-organisation.md | [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/principle--speed-strength-strength-endurance-and-speed-endurance-are-independent-qualities]] | vault + staging (identical); source and target both promoted 2026-10-03 | [[principle--speed-strength-strength-endurance-and-speed-endurance-are-independent-qualities]] (5.1, speed-strength, strength-endurance and speed-endurance as independent qualities, each needing its own means) | review_supertraining_ch5a_opus.md, \"Reciprocal-link suggestions\" |\n| supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--three-methods-for-maximum-strength-repetitive-brief-maximal-isometric.md | [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size]] | vault + staging (identical); source and target both promoted 2026-10-03 | [[heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size]] (5.2.4, when the brief maximal tension method is preferred over plyometrics: explosive force against large resistance) | review_supertraining_ch5a_opus.md, \"Reciprocal-link suggestions\" |\n| supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size.md | [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--three-methods-for-maximum-strength-repetitive-brief-maximal-isometric]] | vault + staging (identical); source and target both promoted 2026-10-03 | [[framework--three-methods-for-maximum-strength-repetitive-brief-maximal-isometric]] (5.2.1, where the brief maximal tension method is defined beside repetitive effort and isometrics) | review_supertraining_ch5a_opus.md, \"Reciprocal-link suggestions\" |\n\n---\n\n## Dropped suggestions (found, but excluded), with reason\n\n- **Bompa `principle--concentrated-loading-overreaching` -> `heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next`** (writer's list, 5c): already linked. The Bompa file links the allow-restoration entry today (it was edited on 2026-10-03). Note that it has no **Related** paragraph; the link sits in its body.\n- **Issurin `case-study--concurrent-training-interference-and-decay` -> `case-study--novice-strength-and-speed-strength-sequencing-experiments`** (one reading of the 5c writer's \"exercise-sequencing and concurrent-training case -> novice case and excess-heavy-strength claim\"): not supported. The novice case cites Issurin's exercise-sequencing heuristic, not the concurrent-training case, and the two are about different things (endurance work blunting swimmers' explosive strength vs the order of strength and speed-strength work in novices). The concurrent case's link to the excess-heavy-strength claim is kept above.\n- **`framework--reactivity-coefficient-and-depth-jump-test` -> `heuristic--rate-plyometric-intensity-by-contact-momentum-from-drop-height`** (one reading of the 5b writer's \"optimal drop-height claim and reactivity-coefficient framework -> height heuristic and intensity heuristic\"): not supported. The intensity heuristic never mentions the reactivity coefficient or reactive ability; it rates intensity by momentum at contact. The RC framework's link to the height heuristic (which does cite it) and both drop-height rows are kept above.\n- **`claim--depth-jump-training-beat-higher-volume-traditional-training-in-jumpers` <-> `case-study--novice-strength-and-speed-strength-sequencing-experiments`, `heuristic--sequence-jumps-then-barbell-then-depth-jumps-across-stages` -> the 475-jumps claim, and the novice case -> `heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size`** (review_supertraining_ch5b_opus.md, \"Duplicates\"; ch5b round 2, \"For the coordinator\"; review_supertraining_ch5c_opus.md, \"Duplicates\"; fixes_supertraining_ch5b_applied.md): all four already linked in the vault. The 5b and 5c fixers applied them before promotion.\n- **`heuristic--isokinetic-testing-limitations-for-functional-sport-specificity` -> `framework--apre-autoregulating-progressive-resistance-exercise`** (writer's list, 5a): not a script row because the source already points at the section in its body (\"see 5.2.2\"); handled as Manual edit 1, which puts the link there.\n- **`fundamentals-of-sprinting--moyer/framework--isometric-set-positions-ankle-knee-hip` -> `heuristic--isometric-apre-six-second-maximum-protocol`** (writer's list, 5a): not a script row. The file's **Related** line is followed directly (no blank line) by a dated `- Added 2026-09-26:` bullet, so the script's `add()` would append this link onto the end of that bullet. Handled as Manual edit 2 (a new dated bullet).\n- **`strength-and-conditioning/velocity-based-training-implementation-guide-from-texas-am` -> `framework--apre-autoregulating-progressive-resistance-exercise`** (writer's list, 5a): not a script row. The source is a Fireflies meeting note (`type: meeting`), not a library entry: it has no **Related** paragraph and ends on \"## Meeting notes (condensed)\", so the script would tack a `**Related**` paragraph onto the end of the meeting notes. Handled as Manual edit 3 (on the \"Daily monitoring\" bullet).\n- **Ch2.3.1 placeholder in `claim--physical-quality-transfer-decreases-as-specific-fitness-increases`, line 65** (\"defer that judgment to later chapters not yet ingested\"): not edited. The book's own sentence (PDF p. 128, printed p. 117) says only that the complex-training claim \"is examined later in this and later chapters\", without naming a section, and Chapters 6-8 are still not ingested, so \"not yet ingested\" stays true. The two 5.1 entries that take up the question are linked from this file by the script rows above.\n\n## Manual edits (not handled by the script)\n\nMake these in the vault (`/Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library/`). None of the three\nfiles is the source of a script row except file 1 (the isokinetic heuristic, which also gets the\nAPRE testing-heuristic row); that edit touches a body sentence, not the Related paragraph, so\norder does not matter. Each Old string was confirmed to occur exactly once in its file. The\nisokinetic heuristic's staging copy under `staging/library/` is identical to the vault today; edit\nit too, or refresh it from the vault, before any later promotion of that folder. Files 2 and 3\nhave no staging copy.\n\n1. **`strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--isokinetic-testing-limitations-for-functional-sport-specificity.md`**, line 62 (end of What it is). Source: writer's list (5a).\n\n   ```\n   Old:\n   authors also name the APRE system (based on DeLorme's original work, see 5.2.2) as an\n   New:\n   authors also name the APRE system (based on DeLorme's original work, see 5.2.2, [[framework--apre-autoregulating-progressive-resistance-exercise]]) as an\n   ```\n\n2. **`strength-and-conditioning/fundamentals-of-sprinting--moyer/framework--isometric-set-positions-ankle-knee-hip.md`**, line 36 (last line of the file, the dated bullet under **Related**): add a second dated bullet after it, in the same form. The file should still end with a single newline. Source: writer's list (5a).\n\n   ```\n   Old:\n   - Added 2026-09-26: [[heuristic--75-85-percent-mv-decision-gate]]: an RTP application of isometric force benchmarks (Nordic and seated calf) as a gate before faster running.\n   New:\n   - Added 2026-09-26: [[heuristic--75-85-percent-mv-decision-gate]]: an RTP application of isometric force benchmarks (Nordic and seated calf) as a gate before faster running.\n   - Added 2026-10-03: [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--isometric-apre-six-second-maximum-protocol]]: Supertraining 5.2.2's isometric APRE, 6-second maximal holds at a set joint angle with 5-10 s rest, set 1 at 50% and set 2 at 75% of the 6SM weight; a load progression for isometric stations like these.\n   ```\n\n3. **`strength-and-conditioning/velocity-based-training-implementation-guide-from-texas-am.md`**, line 27 (the \"Daily monitoring\" bullet under \"## Key topics\"). The APRE entry already links this note (\"daily autoregulation by bar velocity\"). Source: writer's list (5a).\n\n   ```\n   Old:\n   - **Daily monitoring**: Velocity autoregulation fosters responsiveness to individual readiness levels\n   New:\n   - **Daily monitoring**: Velocity autoregulation fosters responsiveness to individual readiness levels (a rep-based counterpart: [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--apre-autoregulating-progressive-resistance-exercise]], Supertraining 5.2.2's APRE, which sets the next load from the reps achieved)\n   ```\n\n---\n\n**Counts:** 68 script rows (supertraining 41, issurin 8, bompa 2, bondarchuk 4, zatsiorsky 3,\nevans 1, moyer 1, myszka 1, research-papers 1, in-batch 6). Manual edits 3. Dropped 8 (one\nalready linked; two writer readings the files do not support; four in-batch links already\napplied by the fixers, listed as one item; three routed to Manual edits; one placeholder left\nas is).\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":657,"cache_read_input_tokens":217540,"output_tokens":23373,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":3110},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":657},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":23373,"cache_read_input_tokens":217540,"cache_creation_input_tokens":657,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":657,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01Q2UQmSiiDH4mg4ZaqdDHjv":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/reciprocal_links_2026-10-03-ch5.md","content":"# Reciprocal links for the Supertraining Chapter 5 batch (2026-10-03)\n\nCompiled from the writers' reciprocal-link lists handed over with batches 5a, 5b and 5c, checked\nagainst `review_supertraining_ch5{a,b,c}_opus.md`, `review_supertraining_ch5{a,b,c}_opus_round2.md`\nand `fixes_supertraining_ch5{a,b,c}_applied.md`. No separate 5c writer list exists on disk, so\nthe Ch1-4 Supertraining sources the 5c writer named were matched to the 5c entries that cite\nthem (each such row's target links its source today). Every pair below was checked against the\nactual current vault file content: source and target both confirmed to exist in\n`Knowledge/library`, each Source resolves to exactly one file by `<book>/<file>.md` suffix, and\nthe source was scanned for an existing `[[...target-slug...]]` wikilink (none found). Each\nRelated text was written after reading both files. Sources are entries that predate Chapter 5,\nwith one exception: the last section holds three pairs inside the batch that the 5a round-1\nreviewer flagged under \"Reciprocal-link suggestions\" and the 5a fixer declined as out of scope;\nneither direction is linked today, so both directions are listed.\n\nAll 75 Chapter 5 entries were promoted to `strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/`\nin SecondBrain commit 3109157 and are in the vault now, so the script can run at once. Its\n`VAULT` constant is the Windows path; on the Mac, run the temp copy\n`staging/_tmp/links_mac.py` (VAULT set to `/Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library`)\nrather than editing the repo script.\n\nStaging trap for the next wave: 45 of the 52 distinct script-row source files (all but Bompa's\nLTAD model, Moyer's wearable-resistance claim, Myszka's donor-sport framework and the Issurin 2016\nbrief) also have a copy under `staging/library/` that is byte-identical to the vault copy today;\nso does one of the three manual-edit files (the isokinetic heuristic). The script and the manual\nedits change only the vault copies. After they run, the vault is ahead of staging for those\nfiles, and any later `promote_to_vault.py --apply` over those book folders would overwrite the\nnew links unless the staging copies are refreshed from the vault first.\n\nLink forms: within a book, `[[slug]]`; across books, `[[book/slug]]`, matching what each source\nfile already uses (Myszka's skill-acquisition entries also use the two-part\n`[[supertraining--verkhoshansky/slug]]` form for their existing Supertraining links). Bondarchuk,\nBompa and Myszka sources use the `[[link]]: text` form their Related paragraphs already use;\nthe Issurin 2016 brief gets a `[[book/slug]]: text` bullet under \"## Related knowledge\", the\nform the Petrakos and Dupuy briefs received in earlier waves.\n\nNo library or vault file was edited to produce this list. No git command was run.\n\n---\n\n## strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/\n\n| Source file | Target | Location | Related text to append | Origin |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| supertraining--verkhoshansky/principle--force-time-profile-determines-strength-vs-power-training-target.md | [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--newton-ii-and-impulse-momentum-as-the-math-behind-the-force-time-curve]] | vault + staging (identical); target vault (promoted 2026-10-03) | [[claim--newton-ii-and-impulse-momentum-as-the-math-behind-the-force-time-curve]] (5.1, Newton's second law and impulse-momentum as the mathematical starting point for the force-time curve; its Fig 5.1 repeats this entry's two-athlete comparison) | Writer's list (5a) |\n| supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--strength-detraining-and-retention-timelines.md | [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--three-methods-for-maximum-strength-repetitive-brief-maximal-isometric]] | vault + staging (identical); target vault (promoted 2026-10-03) | [[framework--three-methods-for-maximum-strength-repetitive-brief-maximal-isometric]] (5.2.1, repetitive effort, brief maximal tension and isometrics; it cites this entry for the point that strength founded on hypertrophy is retained longer; rated contested) | Writer's list (5a) |\n| supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--strength-training-threshold-stimulus.md | [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--moderate-and-mixed-movement-tempo-for-repetition-strength-gains]] | vault + staging (identical); target vault (promoted 2026-10-03) | [[claim--moderate-and-mixed-movement-tempo-for-repetition-strength-gains]] (5.2.1, mixed movement tempos beat a moderate tempo alone for repetition strength gains, Lelikov 1975; it notes that its equal-work Vasiliev 1954 result overlaps this entry) | Writer's list (5a) |\n| supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--two-force-regimes-overcoming-weight-vs-inertia.md | [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--speed-strength-loading-light-loads-maximal-acceleration-and-5-to-1-mix]] | vault + staging (identical); target vault (promoted 2026-10-03) | [[heuristic--speed-strength-loading-light-loads-maximal-acceleration-and-5-to-1-mix]] (5.2.3, loads near 20% of 1RM moved with maximal acceleration, mixed 5 to 1 with loads up to 40%; one of its two levers is exercises in which force opposes the load's inertia rather than its weight, the regime defined here) | Writer's list (5a) |\n| supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--post-activation-potentiation-load-timing-sequencing.md | [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/case-study--arm-throw-height-after-barbell-press-tonic-work]] | vault + staging (identical); target vault (promoted 2026-10-03) | [[case-study--arm-throw-height-after-barbell-press-tonic-work]] (5.2.3, the book's own after-effect case: throw height rose 38-40% after 3 x 3 barbell presses at 80% of 1RM with 10 minutes' rest) | Writer's list (5a) |\n| supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--post-activation-potentiation-load-timing-sequencing.md | [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/case-study--novice-strength-and-speed-strength-sequencing-experiments]] | vault + staging (identical); appends after the previous row; target vault (promoted 2026-10-03) | [[case-study--novice-strength-and-speed-strength-sequencing-experiments]] (5.3, novice experiments on ordering strength and speed-strength work; it cites this entry for the within-session after-effect, yet strength-first and speed-strength-first sessions showed no significant difference in novices) | 5c writer's Ch1-4 sources (PAP framework) |\n| supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--pap-findings-contested-western-vs-russian-literature.md | [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/case-study--arm-throw-height-after-barbell-press-tonic-work]] | vault + staging (identical); target vault (promoted 2026-10-03) | [[case-study--arm-throw-height-after-barbell-press-tonic-work]] (5.2.3, a Russian after-effect result, throw height up 38-40% after heavy barbell presses, which that entry sets beside the Bischke and Morehouse shot-put decline recorded here) | Writer's list (5a) |\n| supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--isokinetic-testing-limitations-for-functional-sport-specificity.md | [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--apre-skill-learning-6rm-pyramid-test-and-rm-muscle-testing]] | vault + staging (identical); target vault (promoted 2026-10-03). The APRE framework link from the same writer item is a Manual edit (item 1), not a row | [[heuristic--apre-skill-learning-6rm-pyramid-test-and-rm-muscle-testing]] (5.2.2, the skill-learning phase and 6RM pyramid test that put APRE, the authors' named alternative to isokinetics, into practice) | Writer's list (5a) |\n| supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--five-criteria-of-dynamic-correspondence.md | [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--dynamic-correspondence-means-matching-motor-characteristics-not-copying-the-sport-movement]] | vault + staging (identical); target vault (promoted 2026-10-03) | [[heuristic--dynamic-correspondence-means-matching-motor-characteristics-not-copying-the-sport-movement]] (5.1, the authors' warning that these criteria mean matching the sport's key motor characteristics, not literally copying the sport movement under load) | Writer's list (5a) |\n| supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--physical-quality-transfer-decreases-as-specific-fitness-increases.md | [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/principle--speed-strength-strength-endurance-and-speed-endurance-are-independent-qualities]] | vault + staging (identical); target vault (promoted 2026-10-03) | [[principle--speed-strength-strength-endurance-and-speed-endurance-are-independent-qualities]] (5.1, speed-strength, strength-endurance and speed-endurance as independent qualities that a gain in absolute strength \"does not necessarily enhance\"; it cites this entry for greater transfer in early training) | Writer's list (5a) |\n| supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--physical-quality-transfer-decreases-as-specific-fitness-increases.md | [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--analytical-synthetic-and-multifaceted-preparation-suit-only-early-training]] | vault + staging (identical); appends after the previous row; target vault (promoted 2026-10-03) | [[heuristic--analytical-synthetic-and-multifaceted-preparation-suit-only-early-training]] (5.1, developing qualities separately and then integrating them suits only early training; it cites this entry's Ch 2 sentence that each quality helps the others and records the tension) | Writer's list (5a) |\n| supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--eight-types-of-muscular-tension.md | [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--two-groups-of-speed-strength-movements-and-their-tension-types]] | vault + staging (identical); target vault (promoted 2026-10-03) | [[framework--two-groups-of-speed-strength-movements-and-their-tension-types]] (5.2.3, which splits speed-strength movements into small-resistance and large-resistance groups and divides the second by these tension types, explosive-isometric and explosive reactive-ballistic among them) | Writer's list (5a) |\n| supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--velocity-and-contraction-specificity-asymmetric-transfer.md | [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--ivanov-1966-contraction-mode-and-which-strength-test-improves]] | vault + staging (identical); target vault (promoted 2026-10-03) | [[claim--ivanov-1966-contraction-mode-and-which-strength-test-improves]] (5.2.1, Ivanov 1966: isometric work gave the largest isometric gain and lowered the vertical jump, only concentric work raised the jump, and eccentric work raised squat 1RM most; the same specificity point from another study) | Writer's list (5a; target renamed in the 5a fixes) |\n| supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--eccentric-vs-concentric-training-adaptation-contradictory-findings.md | [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--ivanov-1966-contraction-mode-and-which-strength-test-improves]] | vault + staging (identical); target vault (promoted 2026-10-03) | [[claim--ivanov-1966-contraction-mode-and-which-strength-test-improves]] (5.2.1, Ivanov's 3-month comparison: eccentric work raised squat 1RM most, 15 kg, while only concentric work raised the vertical jump; one more study in this mixed picture) | Writer's list (5a; target renamed in the 5a fixes) |\n| supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--shock-method-and-plyometric-terminology.md | [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--plyometric-training-coupling-under-0-15-s-feedforward-and-force-time-pattern]] | vault + staging (identical); target vault (promoted 2026-10-03) | [[heuristic--plyometric-training-coupling-under-0-15-s-feedforward-and-force-time-pattern]] (5.2.4, coupling time under about 0.15 s, feedforward and the force-time pattern as tests of classical plyometrics; it cites this entry for the original definition and \"powermetrics\") | Writer's list (5b) |\n| supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--shock-method-and-plyometric-terminology.md | [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--plyometric-training-classification-impact-intensity-and-function]] | vault + staging (identical); appends after the previous row; target vault (promoted 2026-10-03) | [[framework--plyometric-training-classification-impact-intensity-and-function]] (5.2.4, Siff's classification by impact, intensity and function, which replaces the \"classically plyometric or not\" argument) | Writer's list (5b) |\n| supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--optimal-depth-jump-drop-height-for-power-vs-force.md | [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--depth-jump-height-set-by-transition-speed-and-heel-contact]] | vault + staging (identical); target vault (promoted 2026-10-03) | [[heuristic--depth-jump-height-set-by-transition-speed-and-heel-contact]] (5.2.4, drop height set by an unslowed eccentric-concentric transition and no forced heel contact, with no number given; it cites this entry's 0.75-1.15 m range for advanced jumpers) | Writer's list (5b) |\n| supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--optimal-depth-jump-drop-height-for-power-vs-force.md | [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--rate-plyometric-intensity-by-contact-momentum-from-drop-height]] | vault + staging (identical); appends after the previous row; target vault (promoted 2026-10-03) | [[heuristic--rate-plyometric-intensity-by-contact-momentum-from-drop-height]] (5.2.4, Siff's intensity scale by momentum at contact from drop height; it cites this entry's range as the upper limit past which the transition slows) | Writer's list (5b) |\n| supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--reactivity-coefficient-and-depth-jump-test.md | [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--depth-jump-height-set-by-transition-speed-and-heel-contact]] | vault + staging (identical); target vault (promoted 2026-10-03) | [[heuristic--depth-jump-height-set-by-transition-speed-and-heel-contact]] (5.2.4, choosing a training drop height by transition speed and heel contact, a different job from this fixed-height test of reactive ability) | Writer's list (5b) |\n| supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--strength-deficit-sets-training-method-choice.md | [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size]] | vault + staging (identical); target vault (promoted 2026-10-03) | [[heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size]] (5.2.4, brief maximal tension for large resistance and plyometrics for small; it cites this entry for choosing between them by the strength deficit; rated contested) | Writer's list (5b) |\n| supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--match-plyometric-prescription-to-joint-angle-and-fiber-type.md | [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--prestretch-benefit-decay-time-differs-by-task]] | vault + staging (identical); target vault (promoted 2026-10-03) | [[claim--prestretch-benefit-decay-time-differs-by-task]] (5.2.4, the prestretch benefit fades within about 0.25 s in unloaded elbow flexion but lasts up to about 4 s in the bench press; it repeats the Bosco et al 1982 fibre-type finding cited here; rated contested) | Writer's list (5b) |\n| supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--strength-endurance-taxonomy-and-absolute-vs-partial.md | [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--strength-endurance-load-tempo-and-the-75-80-percent-ceiling]] | vault + staging (identical); target vault (promoted 2026-10-03) | [[heuristic--strength-endurance-load-tempo-and-the-75-80-percent-ceiling]] (5.2.5, the strength-endurance prescription, 25-50% of 1RM at a moderate tempo, and the ceiling above 75-80% of 1RM past which endurance cannot be enhanced; it restates this entry's distinctions; rated contested) | Writer's list (5b) |\n| supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--strength-training-raises-haemoglobin-myoglobin-and-aids-distance-sport.md | [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--strength-endurance-hypertrophy-and-cyclic-endurance]] | vault + staging (identical); target vault (promoted 2026-10-03) | [[claim--strength-endurance-hypertrophy-and-cyclic-endurance]] (5.2.5, where squats, presses and good mornings \"will even hinder\" endurance in cyclic exercise; that entry records the tension with this claim; rated contested) | Writer's list (5b) |\n| supertraining--verkhoshansky/principle--equal-force-different-loading-methods.md | [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--heavy-squats-and-light-fast-lifts-miss-the-eccentric-concentric-switch]] | vault + staging (identical); target vault (promoted 2026-10-03) | [[claim--heavy-squats-and-light-fast-lifts-miss-the-eccentric-concentric-switch]] (5.2.4, heavy squats build quasi-isometric strength and lighter fast lifts a large dynamic force, but neither trains the rapid eccentric-concentric switch) | Writer's list (5b) |\n| supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--continuous-vs-interval-circuit-training-for-concurrent-goals.md | [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--traditional-circuit-weight-training-gives-only-modest-mixed-benefits]] | vault + staging (identical); target vault (promoted 2026-10-03) | [[claim--traditional-circuit-weight-training-gives-only-modest-mixed-benefits]] (5.6, the authors' later verdict that the average commercial circuit gives only modest cardiovascular and strength benefits; it cites this entry's CCT vs ICT verdict) | 5c writer's Ch1-4 sources; review_supertraining_ch5c_opus.md, \"Duplicates\" |\n| supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--continuous-vs-interval-circuit-training-for-concurrent-goals.md | [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--continuous-interval-and-mixed-circuit-training-classification]] | vault + staging (identical); appends after the previous row; target vault (promoted 2026-10-03) | [[framework--continuous-interval-and-mixed-circuit-training-classification]] (5.6, re-introducing rest intervals turns circuits into continuous, interval and mixed forms) | 5c writer's Ch1-4 sources |\n| supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--acute-delayed-particular-cumulative-training-effect-taxonomy.md | [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--sequential-and-concurrent-variants-of-systematic-means]] | vault + staging (identical); target vault (promoted 2026-10-03) | [[framework--sequential-and-concurrent-variants-of-systematic-means]] (5.3, sequential and concurrent ways of applying means systematically for a cumulative effect; it cites this entry for particular vs cumulative effects) | 5c writer's Ch1-4 sources |\n| supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--acute-delayed-particular-cumulative-training-effect-taxonomy.md | [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]] | vault + staging (identical); appends after the previous row; target vault (promoted 2026-10-03) | [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]] (5.3.3, a reduced restorative period after a concentrated block, with gains most often after a latency of 1-3 weeks; it cites this entry for the delayed training effect; rated contested) | 5c writer's Ch1-4 sources |\n| supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--acute-delayed-particular-cumulative-training-effect-taxonomy.md | [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/principle--sequentially-introduce-means-of-greater-training-effect]] | vault + staging (identical); appends after the previous row; target vault (promoted 2026-10-03) | [[principle--sequentially-introduce-means-of-greater-training-effect]] (5.3.2, introduce means of progressively greater training effect in step with current fitness; it cites this entry's five generalizations) | 5c writer's Ch1-4 sources |\n| supertraini