pulse-momentum-as-the-math-behind-the-force-time-curve]] (5.1, Newton's second law and impulse-momentum as the mathematical starting point for the force-time curve; its Fig 5.1 repeats this entry's two-athlete comparison) | Writer's list (5a) |\n| supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--strength-detraining-and-retention-timelines.md | [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--three-methods-for-maximum-strength-repetitive-brief-maximal-isometric]] | vault + staging (identical); target vault (promoted 2026-10-03) | [[framework--three-methods-for-maximum-strength-repetitive-brief-maximal-isometric]] (5.2.1, repetitive effort, brief maximal tension and isometrics; it cites this entry for the point that strength founded on hypertrophy is retained longer; rated contested) | Writer's list (5a) |\n| supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--strength-training-threshold-stimulus.md | [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--moderate-and-mixed-movement-tempo-for-repetition-strength-gains]] | vault + staging (identical); target vault (promoted 2026-10-03) | [[claim--moderate-and-mixed-movement-tempo-for-repetition-strength-gains]] (5.2.1, mixed movement tempos beat a moderate tempo alone for repetition strength gains, Lelikov 1975; it notes that its equal-work Vasiliev 1954 result overlaps this entry) | Writer's list (5a) |\n| supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--two-force-regimes-overcoming-weight-vs-inertia.md | [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--speed-strength-loading-light-loads-maximal-acceleration-and-5-to-1-mix]] | vault + staging (identical); target vault (promoted 2026-10-03) | [[heuristic--speed-strength-loading-light-loads-maximal-acceleration-and-5-to-1-mix]] (5.2.3, loads near 20% of 1RM moved with maximal acceleration, mixed 5 to 1 with loads up to 40%; one of its two levers is exercises in which force opposes the load's inertia rather than its weight, the regime defined here) | Writer's list (5a) |\n| supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--post-activation-potentiation-load-timing-sequencing.md | [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/case-study--arm-throw-height-after-barbell-press-tonic-work]] | vault + staging (identical); target vault (promoted 2026-10-03) | [[case-study--arm-throw-height-after-barbell-press-tonic-work]] (5.2.3, the book's own after-effect case: throw height rose 38-40% after 3 x 3 barbell presses at 80% of 1RM with 10 minutes' rest) | Writer's list (5a) |\n| supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--post-activation-potentiation-load-timing-sequencing.md | [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/case-study--novice-strength-and-speed-strength-sequencing-experiments]] | vault + staging (identical); appends after the previous row; target vault (promoted 2026-10-03) | [[case-study--novice-strength-and-speed-strength-sequencing-experiments]] (5.3, novice experiments on ordering strength and speed-strength work; it cites this entry for the within-session after-effect, yet strength-first and speed-strength-first sessions showed no significant difference in novices) | 5c writer's Ch1-4 sources (PAP framework) |\n| supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--pap-findings-contested-western-vs-russian-literature.md | [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/case-study--arm-throw-height-after-barbell-press-tonic-work]] | vault + staging (identical); target vault (promoted 2026-10-03) | [[case-study--arm-throw-height-after-barbell-press-tonic-work]] (5.2.3, a Russian after-effect result, throw height up 38-40% after heavy barbell presses, which that entry sets beside the Bischke and Morehouse shot-put decline recorded here) | Writer's list (5a) |\n| supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--isokinetic-testing-limitations-for-functional-sport-specificity.md | [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--apre-skill-learning-6rm-pyramid-test-and-rm-muscle-testing]] | vault + staging (identical); target vault (promoted 2026-10-03). The APRE framework link from the same writer item is a Manual edit (item 1), not a row | [[heuristic--apre-skill-learning-6rm-pyramid-